WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.430 --> 00:00:04.680
Welcome to "Ear Biscuits,"

00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:07.210
the podcast where two lifelong friends

00:00:07.210 --> 00:00:09.910
talk about life for a long time.

00:00:09.910 --> 00:00:10.743
I'm Link.

00:00:10.743 --> 00:00:11.576
And I'm Rhett.

00:00:11.576 --> 00:00:15.050
This week at the round
table of dim lighting,

00:00:15.050 --> 00:00:20.050
we are talking about our
top 12 creative influences.

00:00:21.010 --> 00:00:23.420
Now we've done top 10 episodes

00:00:23.420 --> 00:00:25.570
where it's like top 10 favorite films

00:00:25.570 --> 00:00:27.870
or top 10 favorite whatever.

00:00:27.870 --> 00:00:30.560
We've done top 10.
Game show television shows.

00:00:30.560 --> 00:00:32.270
We've had this one on the list of things

00:00:32.270 --> 00:00:34.010
to do for a while,

00:00:34.010 --> 00:00:36.170
but I think we've both been
a little bit intimidated

00:00:36.170 --> 00:00:39.820
and the process by which
I kinda got into this

00:00:39.820 --> 00:00:42.910
only confirmed my intimidation.

00:00:42.910 --> 00:00:46.080
So much so that literally 10 minutes ago,

00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:47.580
I was like, "Dude, we gotta make this 12

00:00:47.580 --> 00:00:51.800
because I just feel like
I'm selling people short.

00:00:51.800 --> 00:00:53.370
I don't know how I feel about the 12.

00:00:53.370 --> 00:00:54.870
I don't know how I feel about the order.

00:00:54.870 --> 00:00:56.470
I just, I feel."

00:00:56.470 --> 00:00:58.690
Well, you know what, Rhett?
Almost creatively crippled

00:00:58.690 --> 00:00:59.680
right now.
You know what?

00:00:59.680 --> 00:01:02.480
Hey, let's take a deep breath

00:01:02.480 --> 00:01:05.000
and let's just, let's shed that.

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:10.000
This is just a construct for
us to have a conversation about

00:01:10.010 --> 00:01:10.843
This is good.

00:01:10.843 --> 00:01:12.270
The people that we believe-
Putting me

00:01:12.270 --> 00:01:13.620
in the right frame of mind.

00:01:16.406 --> 00:01:17.903
They've entered our psyche.

00:01:19.096 --> 00:01:22.200
They've impacted us to various degrees

00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:27.200
that then it has made its way into the,

00:01:27.990 --> 00:01:32.080
it's permeated us to then come
out in our own creativity.

00:01:32.080 --> 00:01:36.810
And yeah, it's quite an
exercise to connect those dots,

00:01:36.810 --> 00:01:41.300
but let's just say these
are not definitive lists.

00:01:41.300 --> 00:01:46.300
Let's also say that I
know that I forgot people.

00:01:46.630 --> 00:01:47.640
Oh yeah, and-
But none of them

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:48.870
are listening.
And part of that's gonna

00:01:48.870 --> 00:01:51.010
probably come when you've
got somebody on your list

00:01:51.010 --> 00:01:52.719
and I'm like, "Aw dang."
Hopefully we, yeah.

00:01:52.719 --> 00:01:54.020
We don't know each other's list.

00:01:54.020 --> 00:01:56.240
I've got 15 honorable mentions

00:01:56.240 --> 00:01:57.200
that I'll mention at the end.

00:01:57.200 --> 00:01:58.730
We have not-
Just in case.

00:01:58.730 --> 00:02:00.900
Seen each other's list.

00:02:00.900 --> 00:02:03.208
We're gonna alternate starting with 12.

00:02:03.208 --> 00:02:04.290
I'll go with 12.

00:02:04.290 --> 00:02:07.483
And we're gonna do the
traditional method which is,

00:02:08.353 --> 00:02:09.850
'cause I'm assuming
there's some crossover,

00:02:09.850 --> 00:02:14.850
that if a person says, "My
number 12 is blankety blank,"

00:02:15.457 --> 00:02:16.290
and then the other guy's like,

00:02:16.290 --> 00:02:18.610
"My number two is blankety blank."

00:02:18.610 --> 00:02:20.310
Then you gotta talk about-
The person who's higher

00:02:20.310 --> 00:02:22.380
goes first in talking about it.

00:02:22.380 --> 00:02:23.213
Oh.

00:02:23.213 --> 00:02:25.230
The person who ranks that person higher

00:02:25.230 --> 00:02:27.230
gets to talk about them first.

00:02:27.230 --> 00:02:28.830
But they have to talk about them earlier

00:02:28.830 --> 00:02:29.663
than they were ready to.

00:02:29.663 --> 00:02:31.871
Right, so it's a little
bit of a compromise.

00:02:31.871 --> 00:02:34.610
So you have to do something
you don't wanna do.

00:02:34.610 --> 00:02:38.260
And I'll say that moreso than other lists,

00:02:38.260 --> 00:02:40.160
and it's always been tough,

00:02:40.160 --> 00:02:44.980
that the actual ranking
is much more arbitrary.

00:02:44.980 --> 00:02:47.130
The ranking could be upside
down as far as I'm concerned.

00:02:47.130 --> 00:02:48.500
Really all the way to the top

00:02:48.500 --> 00:02:50.240
because how do you measure these things?

00:02:50.240 --> 00:02:52.800
Yeah, it's how you evaluate
it, which we can get into.

00:02:52.800 --> 00:02:55.150
I think we'll get into those specifics

00:02:55.150 --> 00:02:56.730
as we roll these people out.

00:02:56.730 --> 00:03:01.180
Yeah, there's a difference
between inspiration

00:03:01.180 --> 00:03:04.140
and influence too.

00:03:04.140 --> 00:03:07.210
And so depending on the person,

00:03:07.210 --> 00:03:09.100
sometimes there's a direct connection

00:03:09.100 --> 00:03:13.690
with something that I experienced
or learned from a person

00:03:13.690 --> 00:03:17.420
that then directly impacted
something from our career.

00:03:17.420 --> 00:03:22.420
Other times it's just kind
of the idea of their approach

00:03:22.450 --> 00:03:24.310
or the way that it made me feel.

00:03:24.310 --> 00:03:27.530
It's much more nebulous in general.

00:03:27.530 --> 00:03:28.363
So it's kind of

00:03:28.363 --> 00:03:29.910
all over the place.
Agreed.

00:03:29.910 --> 00:03:31.940
Well, last thing I'll say-
That's true of creativity.

00:03:31.940 --> 00:03:32.773
Yeah, well, last thing I'll say

00:03:32.773 --> 00:03:34.710
before we break into the list

00:03:34.710 --> 00:03:37.210
is that this is not an uncommon
question in interviews,

00:03:37.210 --> 00:03:39.088
like who are your biggest
creative influences?

00:03:39.088 --> 00:03:40.690
And I think that we've always had this,

00:03:40.690 --> 00:03:42.860
or I feel like I've had this,
I don't wanna speak for you,

00:03:42.860 --> 00:03:44.850
I've had this sense that like,

00:03:44.850 --> 00:03:48.170
I don't, like I'm not
formally trained in anything.

00:03:48.170 --> 00:03:50.200
I went to Harnett County public schools.

00:03:50.200 --> 00:03:52.340
I didn't do anything creative in training.

00:03:52.340 --> 00:03:54.093
I went to engineering school.

00:03:54.093 --> 00:03:56.210
I always get this weird feeling

00:03:56.210 --> 00:03:58.200
and super high imposter syndrome

00:03:58.200 --> 00:03:59.860
when you start talking
about creative influences

00:03:59.860 --> 00:04:01.640
because I don't have
a creative background.

00:04:01.640 --> 00:04:04.950
I've just lived and breathed
creativity my entire life.

00:04:04.950 --> 00:04:07.780
And so I don't know what
to say in those situations,

00:04:07.780 --> 00:04:10.050
but when I really dug down

00:04:10.050 --> 00:04:12.570
and started going back
to the beginning of like,

00:04:12.570 --> 00:04:14.860
when did you start thinking
about creative things?

00:04:14.860 --> 00:04:18.160
I started realizing that
there's a lot of people

00:04:18.160 --> 00:04:19.220
who have influenced me.
Well, we have influences.

00:04:19.220 --> 00:04:20.937
But no, and I don't wanna say like,

00:04:20.937 --> 00:04:22.070
"Oh, I don't have influences."

00:04:22.070 --> 00:04:24.920
I'm just saying that I get
intimidated by the question.

00:04:24.920 --> 00:04:27.770
And then even when we
said we were gonna do 10,

00:04:27.770 --> 00:04:30.150
I was saying maybe we should do five.

00:04:30.150 --> 00:04:32.010
And now I've talked you into doing 12

00:04:32.010 --> 00:04:33.763
and I've got 15 honorable mentions.

00:04:35.056 --> 00:04:36.420
It's just a weird process.

00:04:36.420 --> 00:04:37.483
I'm excited to talk about it though.

00:04:37.483 --> 00:04:39.880
I do not want you to mention
your honorable mentions

00:04:39.880 --> 00:04:41.193
because they may be my list.

00:04:41.193 --> 00:04:42.410
No, no, no.

00:04:42.410 --> 00:04:44.970
If you say somebody who's
on my honorable mentions,

00:04:44.970 --> 00:04:47.084
only at that point, will I be like,

00:04:47.084 --> 00:04:48.370
"Ah, they made honorable mention."

00:04:48.370 --> 00:04:49.960
I'm not gonna say that until to the end.

00:04:49.960 --> 00:04:53.993
Whenever I would get that
question of creative influences,

00:04:54.890 --> 00:04:57.010
because it's so hard to nail it down

00:04:57.010 --> 00:04:59.850
and without a prepared
answer or doing the exercise

00:04:59.850 --> 00:05:03.030
that we've only started
to do for this episode,

00:05:03.030 --> 00:05:04.543
I would fall back on,

00:05:06.950 --> 00:05:09.610
I'm not a student of a
lot of the things we do.

00:05:09.610 --> 00:05:11.330
A lot of what we do is kind of like

00:05:11.330 --> 00:05:15.233
we are among the group of people
that invented it, honestly.

00:05:15.233 --> 00:05:20.233
So there hasn't been people in
like the world of creatordom,

00:05:20.270 --> 00:05:23.683
if you could call it, to
emulate, that went ahead of us.

00:05:24.800 --> 00:05:26.180
'Cause it didn't exist ahead of us,

00:05:26.180 --> 00:05:27.880
so if you look at it that way.

00:05:27.880 --> 00:05:29.983
And I always thought of that as an asset.

00:05:30.852 --> 00:05:33.260
It's like, okay, this sets
us up to be more original

00:05:33.260 --> 00:05:34.730
and more ourselves

00:05:34.730 --> 00:05:37.120
and people can emulate us if they want to.

00:05:37.120 --> 00:05:38.840
But really it was,

00:05:38.840 --> 00:05:42.710
I think part of it was just
being intimidated by the work

00:05:42.710 --> 00:05:43.640
that we started to do.

00:05:43.640 --> 00:05:47.140
Who wants to start? You
wanna start with number 12?

00:05:47.140 --> 00:05:47.973
Sure.

00:05:50.720 --> 00:05:54.600
I'm going to start, I put
this person at number 12

00:05:54.600 --> 00:05:59.310
because I felt like putting
them at any number was weird.

00:05:59.310 --> 00:06:01.193
And if they either had to be
at number 12 or number one,

00:06:01.193 --> 00:06:02.480
then I put them at number 12

00:06:02.480 --> 00:06:04.450
because he's likely to understand.

00:06:04.450 --> 00:06:06.127
My dad. Okay?

00:06:06.127 --> 00:06:07.730
So I just started with-

00:06:07.730 --> 00:06:09.410
Jimmy.
My dad

00:06:09.410 --> 00:06:10.360
as a creative influence.
Okay, cool, cool.

00:06:10.360 --> 00:06:12.910
Now I've said this quite a bit about how-

00:06:12.910 --> 00:06:13.743
I totally get it.

00:06:13.743 --> 00:06:16.870
My sense of humor is largely informed

00:06:16.870 --> 00:06:19.080
by the way my dad's his sense of humor is.

00:06:19.080 --> 00:06:21.510
And the things that he thinks are funny

00:06:21.510 --> 00:06:24.650
and the way that he interjects
himself into a conversation,

00:06:24.650 --> 00:06:27.800
the way that he, once he
sort of has an audience,

00:06:27.800 --> 00:06:32.350
the way that he would begin
to entertain that audience.

00:06:32.350 --> 00:06:35.430
I've said forever that
if I wasn't a comedian,

00:06:35.430 --> 00:06:36.390
I would be a professor.

00:06:36.390 --> 00:06:39.680
Well, that's what my dad was
for his entire life, basically,

00:06:39.680 --> 00:06:41.680
starting in like 1980.

00:06:41.680 --> 00:06:42.930
So he had an audience.

00:06:42.930 --> 00:06:44.290
He had an audience every single day.

00:06:44.290 --> 00:06:47.550
My observation, because I definitely think

00:06:47.550 --> 00:06:50.960
he's also a comedic influence on me.

00:06:50.960 --> 00:06:54.010
Like I can, even to this day,

00:06:54.010 --> 00:06:56.560
you'll make a joke and I'll be
like, "That's a Jimmy joke."

00:06:56.560 --> 00:06:57.441
Oh yeah.

00:06:57.441 --> 00:07:01.810
And it's like, if it's dry and a little...

00:07:01.810 --> 00:07:05.640
I want, let's see, how do you describe it?

00:07:05.640 --> 00:07:06.700
It's-
Dry.

00:07:06.700 --> 00:07:11.700
Like not derogatory or judgy,
but it's observational.

00:07:12.430 --> 00:07:13.500
And it might,

00:07:13.500 --> 00:07:17.450
sometimes it might be kind
of like in good spirits

00:07:17.450 --> 00:07:20.193
making fun of something or somebody.

00:07:21.550 --> 00:07:22.810
There's a bit of that.

00:07:22.810 --> 00:07:24.160
And there's a confidence in it.

00:07:24.160 --> 00:07:25.833
There's a confidence.
There's a confidence in his-

00:07:25.833 --> 00:07:26.666
There's a swagger in it.

00:07:26.666 --> 00:07:27.920
In his humor.

00:07:27.920 --> 00:07:30.800
He was good in small groups.

00:07:30.800 --> 00:07:35.800
Like, I mean a classroom is
an awkward size if you're...

00:07:35.930 --> 00:07:39.460
Like, I've never been a standup
comedian, but if you are,

00:07:39.460 --> 00:07:42.370
it's like, when you're
speaking to a big room

00:07:42.370 --> 00:07:43.650
or an arena even,

00:07:43.650 --> 00:07:45.250
when you go bigger,
it's a different thing.

00:07:45.250 --> 00:07:47.380
But when a room gets too small,

00:07:47.380 --> 00:07:48.650
it's like you're talking to people

00:07:48.650 --> 00:07:50.910
in like a doctor's waiting room.

00:07:50.910 --> 00:07:54.010
It's like, that's intimidating.

00:07:54.010 --> 00:07:57.733
Like for us to be behind
a desk on GMM and to know

00:07:57.733 --> 00:08:01.690
that there's a million
people watching every day,

00:08:01.690 --> 00:08:02.910
you just kind of get over it.

00:08:02.910 --> 00:08:06.080
First of all, they're not
there. It's just a lens.

00:08:06.080 --> 00:08:07.710
But second of all,

00:08:07.710 --> 00:08:10.320
that's different than
even my experience now.

00:08:10.320 --> 00:08:15.320
If I'm in like a hangout

00:08:15.880 --> 00:08:17.600
and like you're telling a story,

00:08:17.600 --> 00:08:18.433
and then all of a sudden,

00:08:18.433 --> 00:08:19.780
it's just like-
Yeah, I don't like those.

00:08:19.780 --> 00:08:22.050
The eight people are
listening to your story.

00:08:22.050 --> 00:08:23.410
That's a different vibe.

00:08:23.410 --> 00:08:25.770
It kind of puts you in a different spot.

00:08:25.770 --> 00:08:27.540
But your dad is good in those situations.

00:08:27.540 --> 00:08:28.373
He is good,

00:08:28.373 --> 00:08:30.970
but I think the thing that
I've really taken from him

00:08:30.970 --> 00:08:34.270
is that 'cause I'm not very comfortable

00:08:34.270 --> 00:08:36.900
in those sort of small
and medium size groups.

00:08:36.900 --> 00:08:39.500
Now, I can be, and I can then go into my,

00:08:39.500 --> 00:08:41.250
but I have to kind of shift from-

00:08:41.250 --> 00:08:42.280
It's a mindset.

00:08:42.280 --> 00:08:44.283
Personal Rhett to Entertainer Rhett.

00:08:45.862 --> 00:08:47.520
'Cause a lot of people
might think, "Oh, I bet you,

00:08:47.520 --> 00:08:49.440
when Rhett and Link are
hanging out with their friends,

00:08:49.440 --> 00:08:50.910
they're just funny, funny, funny."

00:08:50.910 --> 00:08:52.743
It's not true. It's just not true.

00:08:53.600 --> 00:08:58.600
Partly because it's a
profession in some sense,

00:08:59.160 --> 00:09:02.200
but there's also a power dynamic

00:09:02.200 --> 00:09:05.900
that comes with being
recognized as the person

00:09:05.900 --> 00:09:09.030
who's currently has the
power to speak to a crowd.

00:09:09.030 --> 00:09:12.290
So a professor has that,
a comedian has that,

00:09:12.290 --> 00:09:13.603
they have the microphone.

00:09:15.060 --> 00:09:17.860
I tend to, that's where I'm at my best

00:09:17.860 --> 00:09:19.653
is when I have the power.

00:09:21.910 --> 00:09:23.967
Not in a negative sense,
but when it's just like,

00:09:23.967 --> 00:09:25.997
"All right, monkey, dance."

00:09:27.407 --> 00:09:28.730
That's when I do well.

00:09:28.730 --> 00:09:29.563
But if it's just like,

00:09:29.563 --> 00:09:31.550
everybody's just kind
of being personal person

00:09:31.550 --> 00:09:32.440
in this situation

00:09:32.440 --> 00:09:34.000
and now are you gonna say something funny?

00:09:34.000 --> 00:09:35.420
You're right, my dad
is really good at that.

00:09:35.420 --> 00:09:36.520
He's good with the quips.

00:09:36.520 --> 00:09:39.280
He wasn't as much of a storyteller

00:09:39.280 --> 00:09:40.982
or there's all types of
genres of funny people.

00:09:40.982 --> 00:09:42.255
He does have some really funny stories.

00:09:42.255 --> 00:09:43.350
He's got some funny stories.

00:09:43.350 --> 00:09:46.990
Well, I haven't heard as much of those.

00:09:46.990 --> 00:09:49.150
It's more of like the one liners.

00:09:49.150 --> 00:09:50.350
Someone else is talking

00:09:50.350 --> 00:09:52.760
and then he'll lean over
and say something to you,

00:09:52.760 --> 00:09:54.340
like give you a little private commentary.

00:09:54.340 --> 00:09:56.333
And he also has things like,

00:09:57.260 --> 00:10:00.210
I don't remember exactly what
he said, but I'll paraphrase.

00:10:01.360 --> 00:10:05.890
Like getting on an elevator
at like a tourist attraction.

00:10:05.890 --> 00:10:08.810
Like okay, 15 people get on
an elevator to go to the top

00:10:08.810 --> 00:10:10.467
of the Empire State
Building or whatever, right?

00:10:10.467 --> 00:10:11.900
And so you're with a bunch of strangers

00:10:11.900 --> 00:10:14.500
and everybody's, no one talks, right?

00:10:14.500 --> 00:10:16.500
So like one minute into an elevator ride,

00:10:16.500 --> 00:10:19.460
my dad will be like, "So how
is everybody doing today?"

00:10:19.460 --> 00:10:22.530
It's like, he'll do
something to break the ice

00:10:22.530 --> 00:10:25.730
when he senses that there's
an awkward situation.

00:10:25.730 --> 00:10:27.170
But he's not one of those,

00:10:27.170 --> 00:10:31.050
some people may do that
because they're friendly

00:10:31.050 --> 00:10:32.120
and they're outgoing.

00:10:32.120 --> 00:10:34.840
He's doing it because the
choice to do it is funny.

00:10:34.840 --> 00:10:36.550
Oh yeah, definitely.

00:10:36.550 --> 00:10:38.210
But he is friendly and outgoing,

00:10:38.210 --> 00:10:39.043
but that's not why he's-
Yeah, he's friendly,

00:10:39.043 --> 00:10:40.320
but the main reason he's doing it

00:10:40.320 --> 00:10:43.200
is 'cause doing this now is funny.

00:10:43.200 --> 00:10:48.200
So as I think about just
obviously genetic influences,

00:10:48.870 --> 00:10:51.767
but also environmental
influences in the way that I,

00:10:51.767 --> 00:10:54.730
and the things that I think are funny

00:10:54.730 --> 00:10:59.010
is so largely influenced
by him genetically

00:10:59.010 --> 00:11:01.300
and the way that he behaved growing up,

00:11:01.300 --> 00:11:02.940
that I gotta put him on the list.

00:11:02.940 --> 00:11:06.250
And it probably makes sense
for him to be number one

00:11:06.250 --> 00:11:08.340
since he's like 50% of my DNA.

00:11:08.340 --> 00:11:09.817
But it's like, I just put him on the list-

00:11:09.817 --> 00:11:11.120
No, he's your dad.
Just so he's on the list.

00:11:11.120 --> 00:11:11.953
He influenced you in a lot of ways.

00:11:11.953 --> 00:11:12.786
He's not an entertainer.

00:11:12.786 --> 00:11:15.797
But like the comedy, it was just,

00:11:15.797 --> 00:11:18.430
it sprinkles in and it
comes out occasionally.

00:11:18.430 --> 00:11:23.430
Like if one of us is gonna be
the one to make the dry quip,

00:11:24.920 --> 00:11:26.370
it's absolutely gonna be you

00:11:26.370 --> 00:11:28.690
and you can trace that to your dad.

00:11:28.690 --> 00:11:32.030
The one liner and the like your
dad didn't raise an eyebrow,

00:11:32.030 --> 00:11:34.933
but he kinda, it was that same effect.

00:11:36.450 --> 00:11:37.773
My number 12,

00:11:39.280 --> 00:11:43.800
I'm hoping to surprise you
with this one with a "Oh yes!"

00:11:43.800 --> 00:11:44.770
'Cause that's how I felt

00:11:44.770 --> 00:11:46.570
when I actually thought of this one.

00:11:47.550 --> 00:11:49.350
Ross McElwee.

00:11:49.350 --> 00:11:50.420
Ross McElwee is-

00:11:50.420 --> 00:11:51.730
Oh, you put him on your list?

00:11:51.730 --> 00:11:52.950
Number five for me.

00:11:52.950 --> 00:11:55.598
Oh man. I thought you would've
forgotten him entirely.

00:11:55.598 --> 00:11:56.670
I got you.

00:11:56.670 --> 00:11:58.848
Not only is Ross McElwee my number five-

00:11:58.848 --> 00:11:59.681
You got me.

00:11:59.681 --> 00:12:02.500
But my rec today comes from Ross McElwee.

00:12:02.500 --> 00:12:04.080
Okay, well let me help you out

00:12:04.080 --> 00:12:06.963
with just kinda giving
some background on the guy.

00:12:08.140 --> 00:12:13.013
He's a documentary filmmaker
from the '80s and on.

00:12:14.440 --> 00:12:18.390
He was first famous for "Sherman's March."

00:12:18.390 --> 00:12:22.540
The longer title is
"Sherman's March: A Meditation

00:12:22.540 --> 00:12:25.520
on the Possibility of
Romantic Love In the South

00:12:25.520 --> 00:12:28.340
During an Era of Nuclear
Weapons Proliferation."

00:12:28.340 --> 00:12:30.690
You're really sealing my
thunder. That's my rec, man.

00:12:30.690 --> 00:12:34.083
Well, I have to give them
context for who this guy is.

00:12:36.500 --> 00:12:41.500
He also released an
autobiographical documentary in 1993

00:12:41.610 --> 00:12:43.137
called "Time Indefinite."

00:12:45.270 --> 00:12:47.890
So I'll let you, why
did you put him so high?

00:12:47.890 --> 00:12:50.340
'Cause yeah, I could
have put him there too.

00:12:50.340 --> 00:12:51.193
Set the scene.

00:12:54.260 --> 00:12:57.207
When we watched that first
film, "Sherman's March,"

00:12:58.780 --> 00:13:01.610
I remember thinking-

00:13:01.610 --> 00:13:02.443
Well, where were we?

00:13:02.443 --> 00:13:06.703
Do you remember when in our
life we were watching it?

00:13:07.880 --> 00:13:10.200
It was like right after college.

00:13:10.200 --> 00:13:14.960
It was, I'm pretty sure it was in context

00:13:14.960 --> 00:13:18.327
of starting to make our own documentary,

00:13:18.327 --> 00:13:19.677
"Looking for Ms. Locklear."

00:13:20.770 --> 00:13:23.040
I think it was the thing
that led us to do it.

00:13:23.040 --> 00:13:23.985
Definitely.

00:13:23.985 --> 00:13:25.730
But I don't think we made the decision

00:13:25.730 --> 00:13:28.650
to do it and then watch it.
We kept referring back to it

00:13:28.650 --> 00:13:30.870
'cause we were getting
into documentary film.

00:13:30.870 --> 00:13:32.530
The thing that we like so much

00:13:32.530 --> 00:13:35.470
that I really connected
with so much about this guy

00:13:35.470 --> 00:13:39.590
is that he made documentaries

00:13:39.590 --> 00:13:44.410
that were incredibly vulnerable,
personally vulnerable.

00:13:44.410 --> 00:13:49.410
They were really a personal exercise in,

00:13:49.790 --> 00:13:52.340
it was self exploration and self-discovery

00:13:52.340 --> 00:13:54.520
through the lens of other people, right?

00:13:54.520 --> 00:13:55.353
And so-

00:13:56.240 --> 00:13:58.600
He was the main character
in his documentaries.

00:13:58.600 --> 00:14:00.003
I call it self-insertion.

00:14:00.970 --> 00:14:04.830
Self-insertion documentary,
which is not a great term.

00:14:04.830 --> 00:14:06.350
Which again is exactly what we did

00:14:06.350 --> 00:14:08.540
with "Looking for Ms.
Locklear" way back in the day.

00:14:08.540 --> 00:14:10.340
Also think "Supersize Me."

00:14:10.340 --> 00:14:14.620
Yeah, the thing that I
love so much about it

00:14:14.620 --> 00:14:18.840
is I'm really drawn
towards documentary film

00:14:18.840 --> 00:14:23.230
where the thing that's on
display is the people first,

00:14:23.230 --> 00:14:27.800
not a subject

00:14:27.800 --> 00:14:29.630
or an agenda or a cause.

00:14:29.630 --> 00:14:31.047
Now I like like political documentaries

00:14:31.047 --> 00:14:32.810
and I like documentaries

00:14:32.810 --> 00:14:34.550
that all of a sudden change
the way that you think

00:14:34.550 --> 00:14:36.490
about the environment, I like those.

00:14:36.490 --> 00:14:38.250
Humanity on display, I totally agree.

00:14:38.250 --> 00:14:41.210
But it's about the
people and that's why I,

00:14:41.210 --> 00:14:43.887
now I didn't put Errol Morris on my list.

00:14:43.887 --> 00:14:45.900
He was an honorable mention.

00:14:45.900 --> 00:14:47.800
I love Errol Morris for
the same exact reason,

00:14:47.800 --> 00:14:51.180
but Ross McElwee is the one
that we first connected with.

00:14:51.180 --> 00:14:52.013
And then the way that he-

00:14:52.013 --> 00:14:53.162
Well, he's from North Carolina.

00:14:53.162 --> 00:14:54.780
Yeah, it was so relatable.

00:14:54.780 --> 00:14:56.480
There was another film, "Bright Leaves,"

00:14:56.480 --> 00:14:59.410
which is about his connection to like the,

00:14:59.410 --> 00:15:00.320
his family's connection

00:15:00.320 --> 00:15:02.820
to the tobacco industry in North Carolina.

00:15:02.820 --> 00:15:05.570
So we had this connection

00:15:05.570 --> 00:15:08.007
and we definitely were referring to it

00:15:08.007 --> 00:15:13.007
and were actively inspired
by what he did in 1986 and on

00:15:13.630 --> 00:15:17.310
when we were in 2003 making
"Looking for Ms. Locklear."

00:15:17.310 --> 00:15:18.900
And not just "Looking for Ms. Locklear,"

00:15:18.900 --> 00:15:23.440
but I would say the entire
"Commercial Kings" series,

00:15:23.440 --> 00:15:26.060
all of our "I Love Local Commercials."

00:15:26.060 --> 00:15:27.900
It was based on that same principle,

00:15:27.900 --> 00:15:29.220
we just love people

00:15:29.220 --> 00:15:31.940
and we love people who've
never been on camera,

00:15:31.940 --> 00:15:34.150
are not prepared to be on camera.

00:15:34.150 --> 00:15:36.990
And actually one of the things

00:15:36.990 --> 00:15:39.860
that I am most sort of
creatively restless about

00:15:39.860 --> 00:15:42.560
over the past 10 years has been the fact

00:15:42.560 --> 00:15:44.810
that we haven't been
able to get back to that.

00:15:44.810 --> 00:15:46.640
Yeah, I'll go one step further

00:15:46.640 --> 00:15:48.400
than just "Commercial Kings"

00:15:48.400 --> 00:15:51.220
and say, again, we were making,

00:15:51.220 --> 00:15:54.670
it was 2003 when we were filming
"Looking for Ms. Locklear."

00:15:54.670 --> 00:15:56.680
Didn't come out til 2006.

00:15:56.680 --> 00:16:00.780
So this is like pre-YouTube, pre-vlogging

00:16:02.150 --> 00:16:06.810
really taking hold in any meaningful way.

00:16:06.810 --> 00:16:11.503
So the fact that he was
autobiographical, vulnerable,

00:16:13.257 --> 00:16:15.710
it was putting his story out there,

00:16:15.710 --> 00:16:18.000
but he was the guy controlling the camera.

00:16:18.000 --> 00:16:19.650
He would set the camera down.

00:16:19.650 --> 00:16:21.777
Then he would walk out and
he would be in front of it

00:16:21.777 --> 00:16:23.000
and he would,

00:16:23.000 --> 00:16:24.920
there would be different
scenes that he would set up.

00:16:24.920 --> 00:16:28.440
We emulated that directly in
"Looking for Ms. Locklear."

00:16:28.440 --> 00:16:33.060
But this is a, it's a vlog
precursor in a lot of ways.

00:16:33.060 --> 00:16:35.670
And so it was more artistic.

00:16:35.670 --> 00:16:39.080
And even though we never,

00:16:39.080 --> 00:16:42.310
I mean, we were very late
and hesitant to vlogging,

00:16:42.310 --> 00:16:46.990
we had this seed planted from
this era in our creativity

00:16:46.990 --> 00:16:49.680
that gave us an appetite

00:16:49.680 --> 00:16:51.400
to connect directly with our audience

00:16:51.400 --> 00:16:53.000
and not just create things

00:16:54.160 --> 00:16:56.860
for an audience to enjoy separate from us.

00:16:56.860 --> 00:17:00.780
So that's why I think he's so important.

00:17:00.780 --> 00:17:01.787
But not, I mean,

00:17:01.787 --> 00:17:03.460
not as important as I
thought he was though.

00:17:03.460 --> 00:17:05.520
Yeah, again, it was, I just put him on,

00:17:05.520 --> 00:17:06.900
I had to put him somewhere.

00:17:06.900 --> 00:17:08.300
All right, what's your 11?

00:17:10.350 --> 00:17:15.350
My 11 is, maybe this'll
surprise you, C. S. Lewis.

00:17:15.560 --> 00:17:18.013
Okay. All right, I get it.

00:17:19.550 --> 00:17:20.820
For you, especially.

00:17:20.820 --> 00:17:22.350
I'll keep this one relatively short.

00:17:22.350 --> 00:17:23.863
So C. S. Lewis, of course,

00:17:26.287 --> 00:17:29.080
he's famously known as
like a Christian theologian

00:17:29.080 --> 00:17:30.480
and an author.

00:17:30.480 --> 00:17:33.210
But the thing that I really
connected with him about

00:17:33.210 --> 00:17:35.030
was "The Chronicles of Narnia,"

00:17:35.030 --> 00:17:38.333
which not a huge fan of the
movies, let me just say that.

00:17:39.260 --> 00:17:40.540
I'm definitely a "Lord of the Rings" guy

00:17:40.540 --> 00:17:42.110
when it comes to the movies.

00:17:42.110 --> 00:17:43.550
And ultimately in adulthood,

00:17:43.550 --> 00:17:45.590
the books of "Lord of the Rings," but-

00:17:45.590 --> 00:17:47.760
It's crazy that they were
like really close friends.

00:17:47.760 --> 00:17:48.750
They were contemporaries, yeah.

00:17:48.750 --> 00:17:49.583
J. R. R. Tolkien.

00:17:49.583 --> 00:17:50.970
I've been to the bar where they,

00:17:50.970 --> 00:17:52.940
I've been to the bar next to Oxford

00:17:52.940 --> 00:17:55.575
where they would hang out.
I remember, you told me.

00:17:55.575 --> 00:17:58.330
So the thing about C. S. Lewis

00:17:58.330 --> 00:18:01.090
is it's the first fantasy
literature that I ever read.

00:18:01.090 --> 00:18:02.290
And it's so approachable.

00:18:02.290 --> 00:18:06.750
It's so easy to read it as
like a 8, 9, 10 year old.

00:18:06.750 --> 00:18:11.100
And I consumed "The Chronicles
of Narnia" voraciously

00:18:11.100 --> 00:18:13.030
at like the age of 10

00:18:13.030 --> 00:18:14.890
and just fell in love with these stories.

00:18:14.890 --> 00:18:15.723
I mean, you can read, I mean-

00:18:15.723 --> 00:18:16.757
It was your Harry Potter 'cause

00:18:16.757 --> 00:18:18.273
there wasn't a Harry Potter.
You can read them

00:18:18.273 --> 00:18:21.263
and you can also read them
in like a couple of nights.

00:18:22.335 --> 00:18:23.960
They're so much shorter.

00:18:23.960 --> 00:18:28.960
And the thing that I love about
"The Chronicles of Narnia"

00:18:29.810 --> 00:18:32.540
is it starts in the real world,

00:18:32.540 --> 00:18:33.950
and this is true of much fantasy,

00:18:33.950 --> 00:18:38.950
but it starts in the real world
and then there is a wardrobe

00:18:39.920 --> 00:18:41.860
that gets you into the fantasy world.

00:18:41.860 --> 00:18:44.390
And that is a concept
that I've never been,

00:18:44.390 --> 00:18:46.730
it just it's like it went into my soul

00:18:46.730 --> 00:18:50.100
and connected with me
on this cosmic level.

00:18:50.100 --> 00:18:52.400
And I am so intrigued by any story

00:18:52.400 --> 00:18:54.290
that follows this convention.

00:18:54.290 --> 00:18:55.970
We're starting in the real world

00:18:55.970 --> 00:18:59.510
and there is a pathway to another place.

00:18:59.510 --> 00:19:01.600
Any movie that does that and does it well,

00:19:01.600 --> 00:19:03.580
I'm all in the whole time, right?

00:19:03.580 --> 00:19:04.413
It's one of the reasons

00:19:04.413 --> 00:19:06.040
that I don't think that
my soul connects with-

00:19:06.040 --> 00:19:06.873
I don't know why the first
thing I thought about

00:19:06.873 --> 00:19:09.420
was "Being John Malkovich," but go ahead.

00:19:09.420 --> 00:19:11.480
But it's also I like secret rooms

00:19:11.480 --> 00:19:13.950
and it's the reason that
we put that weird locker

00:19:13.950 --> 00:19:15.500
in the first season of "Buddy System."

00:19:15.500 --> 00:19:16.673
It's like, "Oh, what if we opened a locker

00:19:16.673 --> 00:19:19.283
and had a secret bike in there."

00:19:19.283 --> 00:19:22.080
I love that convention and
love to put it in things, but-

00:19:22.080 --> 00:19:26.090
Also our novel, "The Lost
Causes of Bleak Creek."

00:19:26.090 --> 00:19:27.080
I mean you go-

00:19:27.080 --> 00:19:27.913
Same thing, yeah.

00:19:27.913 --> 00:19:30.520
You've absolutely got that in the creek.

00:19:30.520 --> 00:19:34.350
And so that way of
thinking about the world

00:19:35.230 --> 00:19:36.637
and way of thinking about-

00:19:36.637 --> 00:19:38.054
Portals.

00:19:39.360 --> 00:19:42.990
Taking what is possible or
taking what is impossible

00:19:42.990 --> 00:19:46.350
and bringing it into the
possible sort of tangible world

00:19:46.350 --> 00:19:48.360
is something that it permeates

00:19:48.360 --> 00:19:50.780
the way that I think about story.

00:19:50.780 --> 00:19:52.040
And that comes from C. S. Lewis.

00:19:52.040 --> 00:19:55.100
I mean, that was the first.
It was in me already, right?

00:19:55.100 --> 00:19:56.817
But he's the one who brought
it out and it was like,

00:19:56.817 --> 00:19:58.630
"Oh, there's a whole world,
there's a whole life.

00:19:58.630 --> 00:20:02.170
There's all these possibilities
that exist in this world

00:20:02.170 --> 00:20:03.003
that you can get through."

00:20:03.003 --> 00:20:05.490
And as a kid,

00:20:05.490 --> 00:20:08.690
many, many times I would be
walking through the woods

00:20:08.690 --> 00:20:10.560
and I would see like a tree
that had like a hole in it.

00:20:10.560 --> 00:20:15.560
And I would be like,
"Okay, is that my Narnia?"

00:20:16.170 --> 00:20:18.107
And I would pray,

00:20:18.107 --> 00:20:23.107
"God, if this is Narnia, let me in."

00:20:23.410 --> 00:20:25.750
Like I wanted to be in it so bad.

00:20:25.750 --> 00:20:26.690
Pray to God.
Yeah.

00:20:26.690 --> 00:20:28.899
Well, I mean, 'cause that
was my way of thinking.

00:20:28.899 --> 00:20:30.457
You pray to Aslan?

00:20:30.457 --> 00:20:33.248
And so C. S. Lewis.

00:20:33.248 --> 00:20:34.343
I don't wanna say anything else

00:20:34.343 --> 00:20:35.530
'cause we got a lot to get through.

00:20:35.530 --> 00:20:39.733
My number 11. Can I
surprise you with this one?

00:20:41.380 --> 00:20:42.940
Robert Rodriguez.

00:20:42.940 --> 00:20:45.050
Okay. He was on the list
and I knocked him off.

00:20:45.050 --> 00:20:45.883
Okay.

00:20:45.883 --> 00:20:46.716
He's an honorable mission.

00:20:46.716 --> 00:20:47.610
All right, whatever.
It was tough.

00:20:47.610 --> 00:20:48.700
That was tough.

00:20:48.700 --> 00:20:53.233
Robert Rodriguez, film director, producer.

00:20:54.460 --> 00:20:57.517
Early films are "Desperado."

00:20:59.100 --> 00:21:02.393
Antonio Banderas and Salma Hayek.

00:21:03.640 --> 00:21:07.477
Really good movie. Super cool.

00:21:07.477 --> 00:21:10.510
"From Dusk till Dawn,"
"Spy Kids," "Sin City."

00:21:10.510 --> 00:21:13.740
I mean, he's even, I hesitate to mention,

00:21:13.740 --> 00:21:16.740
he directed many episodes
of "The Book of Boba Fett."

00:21:16.740 --> 00:21:20.310
One episode of "The Mandalorian."

00:21:20.310 --> 00:21:24.820
Because of his, like,
his Robert Rodriguez take

00:21:24.820 --> 00:21:28.540
on like with the Western gun slinger vibe,

00:21:28.540 --> 00:21:31.720
made him a great choice for that.

00:21:31.720 --> 00:21:33.120
Even though he's getting a lot of backlash

00:21:33.120 --> 00:21:36.870
for "The Book of Boba
Fett" being kind of tepid.

00:21:36.870 --> 00:21:38.450
I'm not gonna go there.

00:21:38.450 --> 00:21:41.370
But back in 1992,

00:21:41.370 --> 00:21:45.540
his first film called "El Mariachi"

00:21:45.540 --> 00:21:50.030
was just a homemade Western,

00:21:52.370 --> 00:21:54.120
but like a gangster Western,

00:21:54.120 --> 00:21:59.070
like a guy was carrying
a guitar case with a...

00:21:59.070 --> 00:22:00.880
Am I getting this
confused with "Desperado"?

00:22:00.880 --> 00:22:02.430
He had a guitar case-
No that's "Mariachi."

00:22:02.430 --> 00:22:04.630
That then he opened it up and it had this,

00:22:05.610 --> 00:22:08.453
it had a gun in it.

00:22:09.620 --> 00:22:11.400
It's been a long time since I've seen it.

00:22:11.400 --> 00:22:16.400
But we discovered him in the
context of Quentin Tarantino,

00:22:18.700 --> 00:22:20.150
who maybe is on your list,

00:22:20.150 --> 00:22:23.900
but I actually replaced Tarantino

00:22:23.900 --> 00:22:26.220
with Robert Rodriguez on my list

00:22:26.220 --> 00:22:30.187
because he wrote a book in 1995-

00:22:30.187 --> 00:22:31.407
"Rebel Without a Cause."
Which we read,

00:22:31.407 --> 00:22:33.177
"Rebel Without a Crew."

00:22:33.177 --> 00:22:34.427
"Rebel Without a Crew."

00:22:35.730 --> 00:22:40.730
And he basically chronicled
making "El Mariachi."

00:22:40.800 --> 00:22:44.650
I mean it has this really
homemade feel to it.

00:22:44.650 --> 00:22:48.210
But he shot it on film and
he like did it all himself.

00:22:48.210 --> 00:22:52.100
He did not have a crew. And he
like didn't even have actors.

00:22:52.100 --> 00:22:56.320
He had these friends
and like acquaintances

00:22:56.320 --> 00:22:58.120
and pulling favors

00:22:58.120 --> 00:23:00.023
to like make this epic,

00:23:01.582 --> 00:23:06.130
super cool vibe, violent movie

00:23:06.130 --> 00:23:09.440
that you could tell he made it himself,

00:23:09.440 --> 00:23:14.250
but the conception and the
creativity involved in it,

00:23:14.250 --> 00:23:18.180
just it overrode the
homemade nature of it.

00:23:18.180 --> 00:23:20.130
And like, you could definitely tell

00:23:20.130 --> 00:23:22.003
this guy was a tremendous talent.

00:23:22.890 --> 00:23:25.920
And given the landscape of
entertainment at the time,

00:23:25.920 --> 00:23:28.630
I believe he got a lot of attention

00:23:28.630 --> 00:23:30.470
if not won awards at Sundance.

00:23:30.470 --> 00:23:31.303
I don't know the details.

00:23:31.303 --> 00:23:33.351
Yeah, that book was
transformational for us.

00:23:33.351 --> 00:23:35.230
Again, he was on the list and I, yeah.

00:23:35.230 --> 00:23:37.950
I mean, it was like 1995, 1996.

00:23:37.950 --> 00:23:39.320
Like we're in high school.

00:23:39.320 --> 00:23:42.033
We're like talking about
going to film school.

00:23:42.033 --> 00:23:45.614
I didn't read it when
it came out, I remember-

00:23:45.614 --> 00:23:47.400
You don't think we read it in high school?

00:23:47.400 --> 00:23:49.870
I remember reading that after college.

00:23:49.870 --> 00:23:50.820
After college?

00:23:50.820 --> 00:23:52.010
That's when I read it.

00:23:52.010 --> 00:23:53.680
So you may have read it
earlier. I read it after.

00:23:53.680 --> 00:23:56.430
I just remember reading
that book and thinking,

00:23:56.430 --> 00:23:58.660
and first of all, we've always been like

00:23:58.660 --> 00:24:00.260
if we've got an idea,

00:24:00.260 --> 00:24:02.300
we're gonna find a way to make it happen.

00:24:02.300 --> 00:24:05.500
And you connect with people
who think that same way.

00:24:05.500 --> 00:24:07.010
And we always felt like outsiders.

00:24:07.010 --> 00:24:08.490
He scrapped everything together.

00:24:08.490 --> 00:24:10.030
We weren't like living in Hollywood.

00:24:10.030 --> 00:24:11.500
We weren't doing the traditional thing

00:24:11.500 --> 00:24:13.440
of like becoming a PA
and working your way up

00:24:13.440 --> 00:24:15.000
and then meeting the director one day,

00:24:15.000 --> 00:24:16.660
we were just a couple of
guys in North Carolina.

00:24:16.660 --> 00:24:19.770
And then you see this
guy who's writing about

00:24:19.770 --> 00:24:22.270
just do it, just make it.

00:24:22.270 --> 00:24:24.180
If you wanna make it
happen, make it happen.

00:24:24.180 --> 00:24:25.670
And there's a couple of
principles in that book

00:24:25.670 --> 00:24:27.220
that really stuck with me.

00:24:27.220 --> 00:24:30.570
The one that we repeat time and time again

00:24:30.570 --> 00:24:32.190
is whenever you're working on a project

00:24:32.190 --> 00:24:34.010
and a film is the most applicable one,

00:24:34.010 --> 00:24:38.710
but after you get the footage,
cut the trailer first.

00:24:38.710 --> 00:24:41.340
Oh yeah, that's where we got that.

00:24:41.340 --> 00:24:43.440
Once you've cut the footage

00:24:43.440 --> 00:24:44.830
or once you've gathered the footage,

00:24:44.830 --> 00:24:46.200
cut the trailer first

00:24:46.200 --> 00:24:48.920
because the trailer gives you this vision

00:24:48.920 --> 00:24:50.300
that you can look forward to.

00:24:50.300 --> 00:24:53.160
You create a trailer that has the vibe

00:24:53.160 --> 00:24:55.520
that you want the film to have.

00:24:55.520 --> 00:25:00.336
And it gives you this true
north to look at because

00:25:00.336 --> 00:25:01.190
you're gonna lose heart.
It makes it real.

00:25:01.190 --> 00:25:03.140
Especially if you're
doing it all on your own.

00:25:03.140 --> 00:25:06.003
I mean, this dude was scoring
his own films as well.

00:25:07.100 --> 00:25:08.430
He was literally doing the score

00:25:08.430 --> 00:25:09.930
to everything back in the day.

00:25:11.512 --> 00:25:13.900
So yeah, we did that for
"Looking for Ms. Locklear."

00:25:13.900 --> 00:25:16.650
And then you also can show
that trailer to people

00:25:16.650 --> 00:25:18.686
and they get excited and it helps you.

00:25:18.686 --> 00:25:19.620
It's a way to get financing.

00:25:19.620 --> 00:25:20.453
Keep going.

00:25:21.400 --> 00:25:26.040
So yeah, there was a lot to
learn there from that book.

00:25:26.040 --> 00:25:27.400
We took a lot of inspiration,

00:25:27.400 --> 00:25:31.810
but it was once we were
making our documentary,

00:25:31.810 --> 00:25:33.053
we were filmmakers.

00:25:33.890 --> 00:25:36.670
We were filmmakers. We
entered it in film festivals.

00:25:36.670 --> 00:25:38.343
We won some awards.

00:25:39.340 --> 00:25:40.740
Got shown on PBS, man.

00:25:40.740 --> 00:25:44.310
Got shown on PBS in South Carolina.

00:25:44.310 --> 00:25:45.200
Hey, I'll take it. I'll take that.

00:25:45.200 --> 00:25:46.442
The under belly of North Carolina.

00:25:46.442 --> 00:25:48.400
I'll take a Carolina.

00:25:48.400 --> 00:25:52.870
Yeah, but we could apply
that label to ourselves

00:25:52.870 --> 00:25:55.341
and we could say we've
done it and we're doing it.

00:25:55.341 --> 00:25:58.760
I mean, we were on Campus
Crusade still at the time.

00:25:58.760 --> 00:26:00.330
We were also that.

00:26:00.330 --> 00:26:05.330
But it's like, "Hey, we have
a film, so we are filmmakers.

00:26:05.520 --> 00:26:08.500
We are achieving the dream."

00:26:08.500 --> 00:26:10.513
And he gave us a confidence to do that.

00:26:11.930 --> 00:26:16.120
And you know, Daniel, the Smosh CEO,

00:26:16.120 --> 00:26:20.950
his previous job was working
very closely with Robert.

00:26:20.950 --> 00:26:22.047
And so maybe we'll have an
opportunity to meet him.

00:26:22.047 --> 00:26:23.540
We could, I'm sure we could.

00:26:23.540 --> 00:26:24.453
Sometime in the future.

00:26:24.453 --> 00:26:25.390
Yeah, he was working at El Rey.

00:26:25.390 --> 00:26:27.840
Be great to shake his
hand. Tell him thank you.

00:26:29.030 --> 00:26:32.450
Okay, we've got 10 more each, but first-

00:26:32.450 --> 00:26:33.283
Oh, this is-

00:26:33.283 --> 00:26:36.820
What are we promoting right
now, Daniel? I can't remember.

00:26:36.820 --> 00:26:38.160
TT2 now and forever.

00:26:38.160 --> 00:26:39.590
Yeah, okay.

00:26:39.590 --> 00:26:41.130
We still want people to
listen to Trevor's podcast?

00:26:41.130 --> 00:26:42.478
Yeah, yeah, yeah. We're
still talking about Trevor.

00:26:42.478 --> 00:26:44.249
What if they're gonna
listen to his podcast

00:26:44.249 --> 00:26:46.038
instead of our podcast?
I mean-

00:26:46.038 --> 00:26:47.092
What if they get hooked on this?

00:26:47.092 --> 00:26:47.925
I don't care.

00:26:47.925 --> 00:26:50.680
And then they don't have room for two.

00:26:50.680 --> 00:26:51.760
That'd be fine.

00:26:51.760 --> 00:26:52.860
That is fine.

00:26:52.860 --> 00:26:53.693
Yeah, I mean,

00:26:53.693 --> 00:26:54.800
it's still a Mythical podcast.
You might like Trevor

00:26:54.800 --> 00:26:56.260
better than us.

00:26:56.260 --> 00:26:58.029
And if you do, there ain't
nothing wrong with that.

00:26:58.029 --> 00:26:59.017
That's a good thing, that's a good thing.

00:26:59.017 --> 00:27:00.800
"Trevor Talks Too Much," check it out.

00:27:00.800 --> 00:27:02.360
It's also on YouTube,

00:27:02.360 --> 00:27:06.207
so you can listen to it with your eyes.

00:27:06.207 --> 00:27:07.970
"Trevor Talks Too Much."

00:27:07.970 --> 00:27:09.110
We need to pick up our pace.

