I've got some faster ones.

00:27:13.820 --> 00:27:17.030
Number 10, Dolly Parton.

00:27:17.030 --> 00:27:19.020
My number 10 is Dolly Parton.

00:27:19.020 --> 00:27:20.890
Hey, we're all happy!

00:27:20.890 --> 00:27:23.480
And we only did this because of Jenna.

00:27:23.480 --> 00:27:24.313
And Jenna's in there.

00:27:24.313 --> 00:27:27.120
No we didn't, but she
might be your number one.

00:27:27.120 --> 00:27:29.410
I dunno, is she gonna
be on your list, Jenna?

00:27:29.410 --> 00:27:30.243
She'd be my number one.

00:27:30.243 --> 00:27:31.783
She's Jenna's number one.

00:27:33.120 --> 00:27:37.200
Yeah, and the numbers are arbitrary.

00:27:37.200 --> 00:27:39.750
So we're neck and neck here. Go ahead.

00:27:39.750 --> 00:27:41.900
Okay, so I don't consider myself

00:27:41.900 --> 00:27:44.930
a long-time Dolly Parton fan

00:27:44.930 --> 00:27:48.170
in terms of when I was listening
to Merle back in the day,

00:27:48.170 --> 00:27:50.390
even when I heard that he had
written some songs about her,

00:27:50.390 --> 00:27:53.497
like "Always Wanting You" was
supposedly written about her.

00:27:53.497 --> 00:27:55.740
"Always wanting you,
but never having you."

00:27:55.740 --> 00:27:57.550
Yeah, he had a big crush on her.

00:27:57.550 --> 00:27:59.760
I always was when I would
hear Dolly Parton song,

00:27:59.760 --> 00:28:02.410
I would be like, "Oh,
that's a great song."

00:28:02.410 --> 00:28:05.200
And I was obviously attracted to her.

00:28:05.200 --> 00:28:08.610
But it wasn't actually until

00:28:09.657 --> 00:28:13.830
"Dolly Parton's America," the podcast,

00:28:13.830 --> 00:28:15.240
was that a Radiolab production?

00:28:15.240 --> 00:28:16.790
Is that what that was?
Yeah.

00:28:18.000 --> 00:28:20.730
That was probably last
year or the year before.

00:28:20.730 --> 00:28:22.890
And it was, it's just
an incredible podcast.

00:28:22.890 --> 00:28:24.360
And I began to really understand

00:28:24.360 --> 00:28:26.080
how much of her story I identified with.

00:28:26.080 --> 00:28:29.010
Now I don't come from the
mountains of Tennessee,

00:28:29.010 --> 00:28:31.713
but I come from the
plains of North Carolina.

00:28:32.680 --> 00:28:33.513
Plains.

00:28:33.513 --> 00:28:37.790
The idea of being someone who,

00:28:37.790 --> 00:28:38.980
because of where you're from,

00:28:38.980 --> 00:28:41.170
there's not a lot expected of you.

00:28:41.170 --> 00:28:44.050
And you kinda feel like every
time you come into a room,

00:28:44.050 --> 00:28:46.370
you are bringing that
expectation with you.

00:28:46.370 --> 00:28:48.640
Now she exemplifies that in a huge way

00:28:48.640 --> 00:28:50.187
because people thought that,

00:28:50.187 --> 00:28:52.180
"Oh, this is this little country bumpkin."

00:28:52.180 --> 00:28:53.150
And she was like, "No, no, no,

00:28:53.150 --> 00:28:54.890
I'm not just some country bumpkin.

00:28:54.890 --> 00:28:57.440
I'm an incredible musician.
I'm an incredible songwriter.

00:28:57.440 --> 00:28:59.980
And I'm an incredible businesswoman

00:28:59.980 --> 00:29:01.590
and I'm gonna have an amusement park

00:29:01.590 --> 00:29:04.170
and I'm gonna have this
place where you can eat

00:29:04.170 --> 00:29:05.727
while people are on horseback."

00:29:07.292 --> 00:29:08.750
And not just that,

00:29:08.750 --> 00:29:11.090
but she is an incredible humanitarian

00:29:11.090 --> 00:29:15.420
who has found a way to be very vocal

00:29:15.420 --> 00:29:18.260
about marginalized people

00:29:18.260 --> 00:29:23.260
and very inclusive as a personal
brand, without alienating,

00:29:23.490 --> 00:29:24.810
which is very difficult to do.

00:29:24.810 --> 00:29:26.670
She's alienated a number of people

00:29:26.670 --> 00:29:27.503
on the right at this point,

00:29:27.503 --> 00:29:30.790
just because she's standing
up for marginalized people,

00:29:30.790 --> 00:29:35.790
but she's found a way to
maintain a very broad audience

00:29:37.200 --> 00:29:40.840
in a way that is unifying people

00:29:40.840 --> 00:29:44.100
in what can be a very, is super
divisive right now, right?

00:29:44.100 --> 00:29:47.690
Yeah, and we seek to do that.

00:29:47.690 --> 00:29:50.080
I mean, to sum it up,

00:29:50.080 --> 00:29:54.733
it's a brand ethos built
around her true self.

00:29:57.000 --> 00:30:02.000
I think that's how I summarize
the creative inspiration.

00:30:02.880 --> 00:30:05.243
Number nine for me is iJustine.

00:30:09.690 --> 00:30:10.523
Oh wow.

00:30:10.523 --> 00:30:11.356
Yeah.

00:30:13.520 --> 00:30:16.053
I have a creator on my list
that's not iJustine. Okay.

00:30:16.890 --> 00:30:18.400
Of course we're friends with Justine.

00:30:18.400 --> 00:30:19.810
We're not in constant contact,

00:30:19.810 --> 00:30:22.027
but we'll go a long time without talking.

00:30:22.027 --> 00:30:24.180
But she was probably
sponsored by Constant Contact

00:30:24.180 --> 00:30:25.013
at some point.
Yeah, that's true.

00:30:25.013 --> 00:30:27.280
And so were we.

00:30:27.280 --> 00:30:30.710
We came up together on the internet,

00:30:30.710 --> 00:30:32.650
but she was ahead of us.

00:30:32.650 --> 00:30:35.870
And I think she was
tremendously influential

00:30:35.870 --> 00:30:39.810
in that she embraced
cutting edge technology

00:30:39.810 --> 00:30:42.050
to set herself apart.

00:30:42.050 --> 00:30:43.343
And we noticed that.

00:30:44.220 --> 00:30:48.000
It was when this website JustineTV

00:30:48.000 --> 00:30:51.820
started making it possible
for you to live stream.

00:30:51.820 --> 00:30:54.517
She was one of the first people to say,

00:30:54.517 --> 00:30:59.517
"I'm gonna live stream my life 24/7."

00:30:59.890 --> 00:31:01.030
I don't know how long she did it,

00:31:01.030 --> 00:31:03.140
but it was, I mean, it
might have, it was at least-

00:31:03.140 --> 00:31:04.770
It was a year.

00:31:04.770 --> 00:31:08.230
It was at least weeks. Somewhere
between weeks and years.

00:31:08.230 --> 00:31:09.640
I don't remember that part.

00:31:09.640 --> 00:31:14.640
But I remember watching her
go about her life as a student

00:31:15.190 --> 00:31:20.030
of what we were coining the
phrase as internetainment.

00:31:20.030 --> 00:31:25.030
Okay, can this, is this even
possible? Is this a good idea?

00:31:25.470 --> 00:31:28.510
We didn't know the answer. We
found it out by watching her.

00:31:28.510 --> 00:31:32.400
She didn't know the answer.
She found out by doing it.

00:31:32.400 --> 00:31:35.620
And she set herself apart.

00:31:35.620 --> 00:31:38.120
She got that edge by
being on the cutting edge.

00:31:38.120 --> 00:31:42.433
And it was something that
we adopted as a strategy.

00:31:43.910 --> 00:31:48.910
And I think that directly led to us doing

00:31:48.940 --> 00:31:51.110
the RhettandLinKast Live, so-

00:31:51.110 --> 00:31:52.323
Pretty definitely.

00:31:53.840 --> 00:31:57.900
We partnered with Ustream,
she was also on Ustream,

00:31:57.900 --> 00:32:01.790
and we did a live internet
broadcast every Thursday night

00:32:01.790 --> 00:32:03.940
from our basement studio in Lillington.

00:32:03.940 --> 00:32:06.400
Which is the precursor to GMM.

00:32:06.400 --> 00:32:08.730
Yeah, that is what became
"Good Mythical Morning."

00:32:08.730 --> 00:32:10.860
We talk a lot about "Good
Morning Chia Lincoln"

00:32:10.860 --> 00:32:12.900
as being the precursor to
"Good Mythical Morning."

00:32:12.900 --> 00:32:15.440
But two guys at one
desk and one microphone,

00:32:15.440 --> 00:32:17.890
that was RhettandLinKast.
Yeah, absolutely.

00:32:17.890 --> 00:32:19.727
That's where it started as a genre for us.

00:32:19.727 --> 00:32:23.893
And it was live for an
hour for an entire year.

00:32:24.790 --> 00:32:27.923
And those archives live exclusively
on the Mythical Society.

00:32:27.923 --> 00:32:29.310
Weekly.

00:32:29.310 --> 00:32:31.308
Weekly, if you wanna see them.

00:32:31.308 --> 00:32:33.391
They're on the Mythical Society.

00:32:33.391 --> 00:32:35.230
But that's where we-
We compared notes too.

00:32:35.230 --> 00:32:39.220
We developed our chops

00:32:39.220 --> 00:32:41.310
of the conversation

00:32:41.310 --> 00:32:43.990
that are reflecting in
what we're doing right now,

00:32:43.990 --> 00:32:45.170
what we do on "Good Mythical Morning"

00:32:45.170 --> 00:32:48.500
behind the desk every day.

00:32:48.500 --> 00:32:50.030
And we didn't have a lot of people

00:32:50.030 --> 00:32:51.970
to compare notes with at the time,

00:32:51.970 --> 00:32:54.565
and she was one of the few that was,

00:32:54.565 --> 00:32:57.280
that we reached out to and
talked to and learned a lot from.

00:32:57.280 --> 00:33:00.700
I've got a creator on my list
as well, for similar reasons,

00:33:00.700 --> 00:33:01.650
a little higher up.

00:33:03.010 --> 00:33:05.543
Number nine for me, Jordan Peele.

00:33:08.000 --> 00:33:09.693
So it's interesting,

00:33:10.660 --> 00:33:15.660
Jordan is a huge influence on two levels.

00:33:16.070 --> 00:33:17.850
The first is just very simply

00:33:17.850 --> 00:33:20.200
before he was the Jordan
Peele that you know,

00:33:20.200 --> 00:33:22.780
when he was just the Jordan
Peele of Key and Peele, right?

00:33:22.780 --> 00:33:23.910
Which you also knew.

00:33:23.910 --> 00:33:25.027
Yeah.

00:33:25.027 --> 00:33:28.420
It's arguably just as visible.

00:33:28.420 --> 00:33:31.610
So I think there's three
aspects that I'll quickly cover.

00:33:31.610 --> 00:33:34.610
The first is the fact that
just as a sketch comedian,

00:33:34.610 --> 00:33:35.860
somebody making a sketch

00:33:35.860 --> 00:33:38.423
that was connecting on the
internet in a huge way,

00:33:39.440 --> 00:33:43.000
both Key and Peele, huge
inspirations for us.

00:33:43.000 --> 00:33:44.850
We could only hope to get to be

00:33:44.850 --> 00:33:48.180
a fraction as funny as
they are in their sketches,

00:33:48.180 --> 00:33:49.980
and even getting to a fraction of that

00:33:49.980 --> 00:33:51.870
would be an accomplishment.

00:33:51.870 --> 00:33:53.570
The second thing of course

00:33:53.570 --> 00:33:57.783
is what he has accomplished
as a filmmaker,

00:33:59.210 --> 00:34:02.460
which is just bringing
comedy and horror together,

00:34:02.460 --> 00:34:05.580
horror, comedy, and
social commentary together

00:34:05.580 --> 00:34:07.723
in a way that's not cheesy at all.

00:34:08.620 --> 00:34:11.010
No one is doing it. People
are trying to emulate it.

00:34:11.010 --> 00:34:12.540
No one has emulated it yet.

00:34:12.540 --> 00:34:15.103
And when he first did it, it was, I mean,

00:34:16.410 --> 00:34:18.643
it was just, it floored you.

00:34:18.643 --> 00:34:21.580
Because it was so good
right out of the gate.

00:34:21.580 --> 00:34:23.020
And it was like, this is-

00:34:23.020 --> 00:34:24.750
This guy's been sitting
on this the whole time?

00:34:24.750 --> 00:34:25.930
Which is the third thing.

00:34:25.930 --> 00:34:29.320
And for me, the most significant thing

00:34:29.320 --> 00:34:33.330
is the transition from a sketch comedian

00:34:33.330 --> 00:34:35.773
to a powerhouse filmmaker.

00:34:36.626 --> 00:34:38.420
And producer.

00:34:38.420 --> 00:34:42.490
In being in this town for the
past 11, going on 12 years,

00:34:42.490 --> 00:34:43.380
by the time this comes out,

00:34:43.380 --> 00:34:45.930
I think we'll have been in LA for 12 years

00:34:45.930 --> 00:34:48.390
and it'll be our 12th anniversary here.

00:34:48.390 --> 00:34:49.940
Being here for 12 years

00:34:50.860 --> 00:34:53.460
and being someone who
is primarily recognized

00:34:53.460 --> 00:34:58.280
as a YouTube person, a
YouTuber, a YouTube sensation,

00:34:58.280 --> 00:35:00.190
you bring that into every room.

00:35:00.190 --> 00:35:02.240
You bring that into every meeting.

00:35:02.240 --> 00:35:05.490
And it's not a good, it's not a plus.

00:35:05.490 --> 00:35:08.990
It's not a plus in this town
to be a YouTube sensation.

00:35:08.990 --> 00:35:10.840
You come in with a bunch of baggage

00:35:10.840 --> 00:35:14.040
that then you have to unpack
and explain who you aren't

00:35:14.040 --> 00:35:15.950
and what you are capable of creatively.

00:35:15.950 --> 00:35:19.690
And listen, we've been able
to do so many awesome things

00:35:19.690 --> 00:35:21.090
and I'm very creatively fulfilled,

00:35:21.090 --> 00:35:24.490
but a lot of the things
that I really want us

00:35:24.490 --> 00:35:25.623
to be able to do,

00:35:26.490 --> 00:35:29.870
the fact that we have a
YouTube presence is actually,

00:35:29.870 --> 00:35:31.610
it works for you in
getting you in the room,

00:35:31.610 --> 00:35:33.170
but works against you
once you're in the room.

00:35:33.170 --> 00:35:34.620
And the fact that-
I think it's true

00:35:34.620 --> 00:35:36.230
of entertainers in general that like-

00:35:36.230 --> 00:35:37.140
They put you in boxes.

00:35:37.140 --> 00:35:39.033
They put you in a box, no
matter what that box is,

00:35:39.033 --> 00:35:40.410
it's really hard to get out of it.

00:35:40.410 --> 00:35:43.090
And the fact that he got out of that box

00:35:43.090 --> 00:35:45.680
in such a decisive-

00:35:45.680 --> 00:35:46.859
And such an Oscar worthy way-

00:35:46.859 --> 00:35:48.276
Successful way.

00:35:49.660 --> 00:35:54.100
Look, again, I mean, that's
not gonna happen with us, okay?

00:35:54.100 --> 00:35:56.010
I'm just gonna be, I'll just
go ahead and break the news

00:35:56.010 --> 00:35:57.570
in case you wanna believe that.

00:35:57.570 --> 00:36:00.190
We're not gonna just suddenly make a movie

00:36:00.190 --> 00:36:02.158
that is going to blow the world away

00:36:02.158 --> 00:36:04.390
and win a bunch of Oscars.

00:36:04.390 --> 00:36:06.980
I mean, I would hope and
that's what we aspire to,

00:36:06.980 --> 00:36:08.780
but chances are that's not gonna happen.

00:36:08.780 --> 00:36:12.080
But just being able to
make that transition

00:36:12.080 --> 00:36:14.040
in the way that he did

00:36:14.040 --> 00:36:18.500
is so inspirational, almost
intimidatingly inspirational.

00:36:18.500 --> 00:36:22.260
All right. I could have
put him on my list.

00:36:22.260 --> 00:36:24.103
Number eight, I'ma go back.

00:36:25.200 --> 00:36:26.770
I think I'm gonna steal your thunder here,

00:36:26.770 --> 00:36:29.630
'cause I'm gonna stay in the
world of internetainment.

00:36:29.630 --> 00:36:33.900
And I'm gonna say the
vlogbrothers, John and Hank Green.

00:36:33.900 --> 00:36:35.683
My number eight. Hold on,
is this your number eight?

00:36:35.683 --> 00:36:36.670
It's my number eight.

00:36:36.670 --> 00:36:38.940
Okay, my number eight is
Hank and John as well.

00:36:38.940 --> 00:36:40.327
So there we go. Putting
them up with Dolly.

00:36:40.327 --> 00:36:42.593
You go first 'cause I just talked.

00:36:44.940 --> 00:36:47.240
They're friends of us, of ours,

00:36:47.240 --> 00:36:49.760
they know how much we respect them

00:36:49.760 --> 00:36:53.750
and it's flattering that
they also respect us.

00:36:53.750 --> 00:36:58.023
Again, with no one to
go ahead of any of us,

00:36:58.980 --> 00:37:02.950
you cling to the people who
are holding on for dear life

00:37:02.950 --> 00:37:07.950
and trying to build an
outpost on the frontier

00:37:08.810 --> 00:37:12.310
of what's becoming the
future of entertainment.

00:37:12.310 --> 00:37:15.370
And for them to have

00:37:16.880 --> 00:37:19.673
a similar relationship as brothers.

00:37:21.970 --> 00:37:24.443
We watched them closely
before we knew them.

00:37:26.120 --> 00:37:29.090
They weren't in the same
room together usually.

00:37:29.090 --> 00:37:33.110
But they had a connection
that was on display

00:37:33.110 --> 00:37:38.110
that then they very overtly fostered

00:37:38.180 --> 00:37:40.043
into a community.

00:37:41.202 --> 00:37:45.140
They named their fans the Nerdfighters,

00:37:45.140 --> 00:37:46.460
they developed an identity

00:37:46.460 --> 00:37:49.060
that was bigger than the two of them

00:37:49.060 --> 00:37:51.820
that they participated in.

00:37:51.820 --> 00:37:56.820
Like a technical, digital community

00:37:56.970 --> 00:38:00.630
of forums and interactions

00:38:01.610 --> 00:38:03.880
through a tool called Ning at the time.

00:38:03.880 --> 00:38:05.900
We're like, "We're just
gonna do what they did.

00:38:05.900 --> 00:38:08.070
We're gonna create a Ning community."

00:38:08.070 --> 00:38:09.840
We're gonna name, yes.
We're gonna name our fans.

00:38:09.840 --> 00:38:10.800
We're gonna let them,

00:38:10.800 --> 00:38:13.210
we're gonna empower
them to name themselves.

00:38:13.210 --> 00:38:14.910
But we were gonna suggest Mythical Beasts.

00:38:14.910 --> 00:38:17.720
Right, and then we're gonna
choose that one together.

00:38:17.720 --> 00:38:21.907
Yeah, so the Mythical Beasts moniker

00:38:23.240 --> 00:38:27.540
was a direct emulation
of their Nerdfighteria,

00:38:27.540 --> 00:38:29.820
which then that becomes
the name of our company.

00:38:29.820 --> 00:38:33.550
And so the branding shifting
beyond the two of us

00:38:33.550 --> 00:38:36.130
is something that we got from them.

00:38:36.130 --> 00:38:40.344
And also how entrepreneurial they are

00:38:40.344 --> 00:38:42.460
in like starting VidCon
and like Hank starting his-

00:38:42.460 --> 00:38:44.760
There was no endeavor that was off limits.

00:38:44.760 --> 00:38:46.567
They're always doing something.

00:38:47.910 --> 00:38:51.300
And then John and now Hank

00:38:51.300 --> 00:38:54.050
having this sort of talent wrapped up

00:38:54.050 --> 00:38:55.610
in being able to write

00:38:55.610 --> 00:38:58.470
that they're kind of sitting on as well,

00:38:58.470 --> 00:39:00.240
and then unleashing that in the world

00:39:00.240 --> 00:39:03.460
in a really significant way that isn't,

00:39:03.460 --> 00:39:05.920
they're being recognized
for their accomplishments,

00:39:05.920 --> 00:39:07.410
not because they had a YouTube channel,

00:39:07.410 --> 00:39:10.183
but because they're genuinely
talented at writing.

00:39:11.300 --> 00:39:16.300
And again, that's just, that's
inspirational to me as well.

00:39:16.770 --> 00:39:18.493
Number seven.

00:39:20.377 --> 00:39:24.920
I feel bad not, I feel bad
putting this man on the list

00:39:24.920 --> 00:39:27.010
in a place that isn't of preeminence,

00:39:27.010 --> 00:39:30.350
but Merle Haggard is my number seven.

00:39:30.350 --> 00:39:32.020
Now I-
My number seven

00:39:32.020 --> 00:39:33.226
is Merle Haggard.

00:39:33.226 --> 00:39:34.910
Oh really?

00:39:34.910 --> 00:39:35.943
Yeah, I guess we're thinking
the same thing here.

00:39:35.943 --> 00:39:38.417
It's crazy how this ended up happening.

00:39:38.417 --> 00:39:41.030
And there's, okay, I'll
let you couch this one

00:39:41.030 --> 00:39:44.520
because there's different,
this is a tough one.

00:39:44.520 --> 00:39:45.870
Well, I'll just be honest with you.

00:39:45.870 --> 00:39:49.070
There was a point in which
Merle was not on the list

00:39:49.070 --> 00:39:52.130
because when I was thinking
about him, I was like-

00:39:52.130 --> 00:39:54.023
We're not country music artists.

00:39:55.439 --> 00:39:58.130
I absolutely love his music,

00:39:58.130 --> 00:40:00.913
but from his creative influence,

00:40:02.960 --> 00:40:04.480
what kind of creative
influence has he had?

00:40:04.480 --> 00:40:05.640
Is that how I think about Merle?

00:40:05.640 --> 00:40:06.990
Or just I think about this guy that just,

00:40:06.990 --> 00:40:09.290
I love the way he does what he does.

00:40:09.290 --> 00:40:13.530
But then I started realizing,
oh no, like I've sung...

00:40:13.530 --> 00:40:14.363
And we talked about it.

00:40:14.363 --> 00:40:17.180
We were recently on a ski
trip together with our boys.

00:40:17.180 --> 00:40:18.013
We were coming back down,

00:40:18.013 --> 00:40:20.760
well, actually, as we were
going up there, I think.

00:40:20.760 --> 00:40:22.600
No, coming back down,
we listened to Merle,

00:40:22.600 --> 00:40:24.870
a playlist that you put together.

00:40:24.870 --> 00:40:26.034
Which I asked you to share with me

00:40:26.034 --> 00:40:27.400
and you still haven't shared
it with me, by the way.

00:40:27.400 --> 00:40:28.727
Yeah, I wanted you to ask me again.

00:40:28.727 --> 00:40:32.470
But it's basically the best
Merle throughout the years,

00:40:32.470 --> 00:40:35.697
which we pretty much agree
exactly on what that is.

00:40:35.697 --> 00:40:37.950
And I just don't, I'm not
a guy who makes playlists

00:40:37.950 --> 00:40:39.490
and so I need you to make them for me

00:40:39.490 --> 00:40:41.267
so I can then enjoy them.

00:40:41.267 --> 00:40:43.403
But in listening to that playlist,

00:40:44.890 --> 00:40:47.570
one of the things you said
was this stuff is just,

00:40:47.570 --> 00:40:49.570
so we listened to this so much

00:40:49.570 --> 00:40:51.830
because we grew up in a different era.

00:40:51.830 --> 00:40:53.650
We grew up in a time where you had tapes

00:40:53.650 --> 00:40:56.510
and it was the tapes that
you had that you played.

00:40:56.510 --> 00:40:58.350
And so it was like

00:40:59.320 --> 00:41:03.040
building 10 Spotify playlists

00:41:03.040 --> 00:41:05.620
and never departing from them
for like a 10-year period.

00:41:05.620 --> 00:41:07.250
If you were to do that,

00:41:07.250 --> 00:41:08.677
you think about the way that music

00:41:08.677 --> 00:41:11.550
and the person behind that music
would seep into your bones.

00:41:11.550 --> 00:41:13.190
You couldn't even shuffle it.

00:41:13.190 --> 00:41:14.060
Yeah. Right.

00:41:14.060 --> 00:41:16.620
And so the way, first of all,

00:41:16.620 --> 00:41:19.297
the way that we sing is
heavily influenced by Merle.

00:41:19.297 --> 00:41:20.670
Yeah, the way-

00:41:20.670 --> 00:41:22.310
The way we think about melody

00:41:22.310 --> 00:41:25.040
is heavily influenced by Merle Haggard.

00:41:25.040 --> 00:41:28.520
And so that, and music is
a big part of what we do

00:41:29.590 --> 00:41:34.250
and has been even a bigger
part of what we do in the past.

00:41:34.250 --> 00:41:37.850
So it isn't that there's
something that Merle has done.

00:41:37.850 --> 00:41:40.850
This is more of the indirect
influence sort of thing.

00:41:40.850 --> 00:41:43.470
The like you're around
somebody and it seeps into,

00:41:43.470 --> 00:41:44.750
and you know that it's influencing

00:41:44.750 --> 00:41:45.990
the way you go about things.

00:41:45.990 --> 00:41:47.750
I think I can make it
a little more direct.

00:41:47.750 --> 00:41:52.730
I think that we would pick songs of his

00:41:52.730 --> 00:41:55.750
and we would dissect them.

00:41:55.750 --> 00:42:00.200
I think there was like an
art appreciation aspect to,

00:42:00.200 --> 00:42:01.180
we would sit in the car,

00:42:01.180 --> 00:42:05.410
we would pick it apart and
appreciate parts of it.

00:42:05.410 --> 00:42:10.110
So we never had like
art appreciation classes

00:42:10.110 --> 00:42:15.110
to really understand the
value of how art can move you.

00:42:15.210 --> 00:42:18.780
It just wasn't an emphasis
of ours in high school,

00:42:18.780 --> 00:42:21.200
but it was something that we
found ourselves doing impromptu

00:42:21.200 --> 00:42:22.960
through his music.

00:42:22.960 --> 00:42:26.470
And we would marvel at the subject matters

00:42:26.470 --> 00:42:31.470
that he would choose to,
the pictures he would paint.

00:42:31.900 --> 00:42:33.947
And how we would say things like,

00:42:33.947 --> 00:42:37.717
"There's nobody else writing
songs like this on the radio."

00:42:38.800 --> 00:42:40.660
There was an innovation in our minds.

00:42:40.660 --> 00:42:44.960
There was a, it was unique.

00:42:44.960 --> 00:42:46.628
And the choices that he was making,

00:42:46.628 --> 00:42:47.917
the thing that we talk about a lot is-

00:42:47.917 --> 00:42:49.320
It was self-expression.

00:42:49.320 --> 00:42:50.153
Well, in the way that-
It was different.

00:42:50.153 --> 00:42:51.920
In the way that he brought,

00:42:51.920 --> 00:42:53.870
like when he gets into
the late '70s, early '80s,

00:42:53.870 --> 00:42:55.393
which is our favorite era.

00:42:56.734 --> 00:42:57.567
And just, and I'm also,

00:42:57.567 --> 00:42:59.640
I'm beginning to say era instead
of era, just so you know.

00:42:59.640 --> 00:43:01.830
Okay, you sound, I guess, smarter.

00:43:01.830 --> 00:43:03.230
Just because era sounds like

00:43:03.230 --> 00:43:05.420
you might be saying error, error.

00:43:05.420 --> 00:43:06.670
Era, okay do it.
So era,

00:43:06.670 --> 00:43:07.503
just so you know.
All right.

00:43:07.503 --> 00:43:09.330
I think it's like the official

00:43:09.330 --> 00:43:11.193
New York Times pronunciation.

00:43:12.200 --> 00:43:13.033
I don't know.

00:43:13.033 --> 00:43:14.210
How did Merle say it?

00:43:14.210 --> 00:43:15.080
Era.

00:43:15.080 --> 00:43:16.780
No, no he didn't.

00:43:16.780 --> 00:43:20.260
So the thing that he was doing at the time

00:43:20.260 --> 00:43:23.670
is the choices he was making
musically with instrumentation,

00:43:23.670 --> 00:43:25.200
the fact that he was like bringing in

00:43:25.200 --> 00:43:27.250
this jazz influence outta nowhere

00:43:27.250 --> 00:43:29.390
and all of a sudden there was
a saxophone in country music

00:43:29.390 --> 00:43:31.660
and no one was doing that kind of thing.

00:43:31.660 --> 00:43:36.400
It wasn't like when you listen to music

00:43:36.400 --> 00:43:37.253
and you're just like,

00:43:37.253 --> 00:43:38.850
"I just like the way this makes me feel,

00:43:38.850 --> 00:43:40.480
and this is just a cool vibe."

00:43:40.480 --> 00:43:43.820
It was that, but then it
was just like, oh man,

00:43:43.820 --> 00:43:47.750
this was an exercise in curation

00:43:47.750 --> 00:43:50.190
when he was making these songs.

00:43:50.190 --> 00:43:53.520
The way that he brought these
specific people together

00:43:53.520 --> 00:43:56.670
to make this specific
sound and you just let...

00:43:56.670 --> 00:43:58.200
Just the other day when we were
listening to that playlist,

00:43:58.200 --> 00:44:02.340
I was just reminded, I'm
like, "No one has done this."

00:44:02.340 --> 00:44:03.724
The singularity of his voice.

00:44:03.724 --> 00:44:06.180
The way he's done it.
And how we could study

00:44:06.180 --> 00:44:07.513
its development,

00:44:08.420 --> 00:44:12.490
helped us understand the
identity of an artist.

00:44:12.490 --> 00:44:15.693
You know what, I wanna take an aside in,

00:44:16.950 --> 00:44:18.560
on the ski trip coming back.

00:44:18.560 --> 00:44:21.870
I had made that playlist
like months earlier

00:44:21.870 --> 00:44:24.067
and I was just laying in
my bed and I was like,

00:44:24.067 --> 00:44:27.660
"I'm gonna take all of my
favorite Merle Haggard songs

00:44:27.660 --> 00:44:29.850
and I'm gonna make a playlist."

00:44:29.850 --> 00:44:34.290
And so without, we know all
of his discography so well

00:44:34.290 --> 00:44:36.090
that like without even listening to him,

00:44:36.090 --> 00:44:37.460
I could go album by album

00:44:37.460 --> 00:44:42.090
and just pick the songs
that like are just magical.

00:44:42.090 --> 00:44:44.020
That's what I called the playlist,

00:44:44.020 --> 00:44:45.400
Magical Merle.
Magical Merle.

00:44:45.400 --> 00:44:46.290
Oh, that sounds like something

00:44:46.290 --> 00:44:47.630
we would have written on a cassette.

00:44:47.630 --> 00:44:49.160
Exactly.
In 10th grade.

00:44:49.160 --> 00:44:53.930
And you know what? I never
listened to the playlist.

00:44:53.930 --> 00:44:55.880
And then when we went skiing-

00:44:55.880 --> 00:44:56.863
You never listened to that playlist?

00:44:56.863 --> 00:44:58.893
I never went back and listened to it.

00:44:59.963 --> 00:45:03.350
And it's probably been nine
months since I made it.

00:45:03.350 --> 00:45:05.330
And so when we were coming back down,

00:45:05.330 --> 00:45:07.440
we skiing with the boys

00:45:07.440 --> 00:45:10.430
and then we were driving
back that afternoon.

00:45:10.430 --> 00:45:13.390
I got in the car and I was
like, I thought of the playlist.

00:45:13.390 --> 00:45:15.630
And I was like, "This will be perfect.

00:45:15.630 --> 00:45:17.260
I'm gonna start playing this thing."

00:45:17.260 --> 00:45:18.860
And I didn't tell you any of this,

00:45:18.860 --> 00:45:21.230
but I started playing the,

00:45:21.230 --> 00:45:24.770
I said, "I made this playlist,
this Merle playlist."

00:45:24.770 --> 00:45:27.440
And that's all I said,
and I started playing it.

00:45:27.440 --> 00:45:32.190
And we drove all the
way down the mountain.

00:45:32.190 --> 00:45:36.091
Absolutely beautiful sunset

00:45:36.091 --> 00:45:38.110
coming from Big Bear on what's it,

00:45:38.110 --> 00:45:40.800
the Rim of the World road.

00:45:40.800 --> 00:45:43.190
You actually, for those
of you in California,

00:45:43.190 --> 00:45:45.660
you don't come down to
the 330 to San Bernardino.

00:45:45.660 --> 00:45:47.000
You stay on the mountain

00:45:47.000 --> 00:45:49.120
and go past Lake Arrowhead and stay.

00:45:49.120 --> 00:45:50.750
It's called the Rim of the World.

00:45:50.750 --> 00:45:52.800
And if the sun is setting-
It's crazy.

00:45:52.800 --> 00:45:54.017
It's amazing and once the sun sets-

00:45:54.017 --> 00:45:55.740
It's like an incredible route.

00:45:55.740 --> 00:45:59.190
You can see like all of Los Angeles.

00:45:59.190 --> 00:46:02.683
And then when the light's in,
you know that's the coastline.

00:46:04.070 --> 00:46:05.490
And it was so beautiful.

00:46:05.490 --> 00:46:07.520
And to me, it was the,

00:46:07.520 --> 00:46:12.310
the boys were on their phones
or falling asleep in the back.

00:46:12.310 --> 00:46:14.170
Exhausted from skiing.

00:46:14.170 --> 00:46:19.170
And it was a flashback to
when we were in high school

00:46:19.670 --> 00:46:22.550
and we would just sit there in the car,

00:46:22.550 --> 00:46:26.130
drive around aimlessly, and
listen to these Merle mix tapes

00:46:26.130 --> 00:46:28.320
that we would make for each other.

00:46:28.320 --> 00:46:33.320
And it was a pristine moment.

00:46:33.400 --> 00:46:37.720
And at a certain point,
we get down the mountain,

00:46:37.720 --> 00:46:39.910
you fell asleep a little
bit, you wake back up,

00:46:39.910 --> 00:46:41.530
the playlist is still going.

00:46:41.530 --> 00:46:43.720
We're like, we're not
really talking that much,

00:46:43.720 --> 00:46:45.530
but when we were, we were usually talking

00:46:45.530 --> 00:46:46.830
about the music.
About Merle.

00:46:46.830 --> 00:46:48.710
Which is what we would do.

00:46:48.710 --> 00:46:51.970
And it had been, I mean,
it had been so long

00:46:51.970 --> 00:46:53.040
since we had done that.

00:46:53.040 --> 00:46:56.150
It was like, I just remember
thinking, "This is perfect.

00:46:56.150 --> 00:46:59.480
This is a special friendship moment."

00:46:59.480 --> 00:47:04.480
And we got back to my
house where you had parked

00:47:04.810 --> 00:47:06.050
and we were gonna part ways,

00:47:06.050 --> 00:47:09.073
and it was basically
the end of the playlist.

00:47:10.250 --> 00:47:12.410
This is when it got back
to like the '60s stuff.

00:47:12.410 --> 00:47:16.340
Yeah, stuff that I
wasn't as interested in.

00:47:16.340 --> 00:47:18.870
So the playlist came to an end, basically.

00:47:18.870 --> 00:47:21.980
It was perfectly timed for this
trip. I didn't even know it.

00:47:21.980 --> 00:47:24.957
And at the one point
where like you woke up

00:47:24.957 --> 00:47:27.370
and it was still going and
we were talking about it

00:47:27.370 --> 00:47:31.980
and you said, "Send me this playlist."

00:47:31.980 --> 00:47:32.850
But you didn't.

00:47:32.850 --> 00:47:34.540
I gotta tell you,

00:47:34.540 --> 00:47:39.170
I realized at that moment
that that is my love language.

00:47:39.170 --> 00:47:40.180
Making playlists?

00:47:40.180 --> 00:47:45.180
No, somebody saying unprompted,
"Hey, what is this?"

00:47:46.430 --> 00:47:47.500
And it's happened a couple other times.

00:47:47.500 --> 00:47:50.490
They're like, "What's this
music that you're playing here?"

00:47:50.490 --> 00:47:52.500
I was like, "Oh, it's a playlist I made."

00:47:52.500 --> 00:47:54.627
And then a few days later, get a text.

00:47:54.627 --> 00:47:57.030
"Hey, can you send me
that playlist you made?"

00:47:57.030 --> 00:47:58.630
That is my love language.

00:47:58.630 --> 00:47:59.463
So you're a DJ.

00:47:59.463 --> 00:48:00.884
So yeah, I'm a DJ.

00:48:00.884 --> 00:48:02.770
I mean like really you
missed your calling.

00:48:02.770 --> 00:48:05.300
You shouldn't be a hair
stylist. You should be a DJ.

00:48:05.300 --> 00:48:08.800
But the tie to the fact that like,

00:48:08.800 --> 00:48:10.940
without either one of us talking about

00:48:10.940 --> 00:48:13.340
what I'm saying right now,

00:48:13.340 --> 00:48:16.157
like to me that was your way of saying,

00:48:16.157 --> 00:48:18.910
"Hey, this resonated with me.

00:48:18.910 --> 00:48:21.810
This means something to
me too. This took me back.

00:48:21.810 --> 00:48:23.110
Send me that playlist."

00:48:23.110 --> 00:48:27.150
Yeah, because the Merle
discography is so big,

00:48:27.150 --> 00:48:28.030
I just find myself,

00:48:28.030 --> 00:48:29.260
there's like a couple of albums

00:48:29.260 --> 00:48:30.690
that I'll bring up on Spotify,

00:48:30.690 --> 00:48:32.130
and every single playlist

00:48:32.130 --> 00:48:34.210
that people have made on Spotify sucks

00:48:34.210 --> 00:48:35.860
because they always put
"Okie from Muskogee"

00:48:35.860 --> 00:48:37.150
as like the first song.

00:48:37.150 --> 00:48:39.109
There's two reasons I
haven't sent it to you.

00:48:39.109 --> 00:48:40.890
People don't understand Merle Haggard,

00:48:40.890 --> 00:48:44.260
like 99% of people who like
him don't understand him

00:48:44.260 --> 00:48:45.593
is my contention.

00:48:47.640 --> 00:48:50.617
But we do, and it's something
special that we had.

00:48:50.617 --> 00:48:51.450
That's right.

00:48:51.450 --> 00:48:54.140
And that we relived in that moment.

00:48:54.140 --> 00:48:56.927
So hey, it was a very special moment.

00:48:56.927 --> 00:48:59.870
It was very special, but it
would be even more special

00:48:59.870 --> 00:49:01.330
if you would just send me the playlist.

00:49:01.330 --> 00:49:03.030
The reason why I didn't send it

00:49:03.030 --> 00:49:08.030
is 'cause I'm thinking
about tweaking the order.

00:49:08.480 --> 00:49:10.370
But the other reason is-

00:49:10.370 --> 00:49:11.280
Well, you could tweak the order

00:49:11.280 --> 00:49:12.430
and just keep sharing it with me.

00:49:12.430 --> 00:49:14.230
It'll update automatically.
Okay, that's true.

00:49:14.230 --> 00:49:16.660
I wanted you to ask me again

00:49:16.660 --> 00:49:18.840
because it made me feel
so good when you asked me.

00:49:18.840 --> 00:49:20.560
I was like-
That's a little selfish.

00:49:20.560 --> 00:49:21.750
Yeah, it was.
That's a little selfish.

00:49:21.750 --> 00:49:23.000
All right, I'll send it to you.

00:49:23.000 --> 00:49:25.660
And you know what? I will
also share it with you.

00:49:25.660 --> 00:49:26.833
I will tweet it out.

00:49:28.292 --> 00:49:32.090
So go to my Twitter. It'll
lead you to my Spotify.

00:49:32.090 --> 00:49:32.960
I'll tweet it out.

00:49:32.960 --> 00:49:34.390
And then if it changes over time,

00:49:34.390 --> 00:49:37.810
it's 'cause I'm adding, I'm optimizing it.

00:49:37.810 --> 00:49:39.000
Magical Merle.

00:49:39.000 --> 00:49:42.890
Number six, right?

00:49:42.890 --> 00:49:45.950
Yeah. What's your number six?

00:49:45.950 --> 00:49:48.043
Number six is Flight of the Concords.

00:49:49.570 --> 00:49:54.230
Oh, I didn't think about them.

00:49:54.230 --> 00:49:55.410
Really?

00:49:55.410 --> 00:49:56.320
Yes.

00:49:56.320 --> 00:49:58.730
Okay, this is great. Glad you did.

00:49:58.730 --> 00:50:01.300
Okay, so if you're not familiar

00:50:01.300 --> 00:50:03.363
with Flight of the Concords, I'm sorry,

00:50:04.750 --> 00:50:07.770
but this is Brett and Jemaine.

00:50:07.770 --> 00:50:09.910
Jemaine Clement who,

00:50:09.910 --> 00:50:11.920
lots of people call him Jermaine Clement,

00:50:11.920 --> 00:50:14.670
but it's Jemaine Clement,
which I guess is a Kiwi name.

00:50:16.930 --> 00:50:20.150
So these are New Zealanders
that are kind of in the,

00:50:20.150 --> 00:50:21.960
Jemaine has gone on to become an actor

00:50:21.960 --> 00:50:24.960
that you would recognize
from many different movies.

00:50:24.960 --> 00:50:26.140
You might recognize Bret

00:50:26.140 --> 00:50:28.470
from "Lord of the Rings:
Fellowship of the Ring"

00:50:28.470 --> 00:50:30.930
as an elf, but just as a very brief cameo-

00:50:30.930 --> 00:50:32.070
I mean he won an Oscar

00:50:32.070 --> 00:50:34.260
for writing the music for the Muppet-

00:50:34.260 --> 00:50:35.500
The Muppet movie, yeah.

00:50:35.500 --> 00:50:37.870
So he's kind of gone into
the songwriting thing

00:50:37.870 --> 00:50:40.940
and I think Jemaine is
more doing the acting thing

00:50:40.940 --> 00:50:42.810
and producing thing.
Jemaine is a co-creator of-

00:50:42.810 --> 00:50:44.510
"What We Do in the Shadows."

00:50:44.510 --> 00:50:46.697
So Jemaine was in "What
We Do in the Shadows,"

00:50:46.697 --> 00:50:48.820
and so he's collaborating
with Taika Waititi

00:50:48.820 --> 00:50:51.179
who didn't make my list,
but is an honorable mention.

00:50:51.179 --> 00:50:52.866
Ooh yes.

00:50:52.866 --> 00:50:55.547
I love Taika, but he's
more of a recent influence,

00:50:55.547 --> 00:50:58.117
and I only know about
him through Jemaine, so.

00:50:58.117 --> 00:51:01.079
And that comedic tone, that Kiwi tone

00:51:01.079 --> 00:51:03.050
is something that it just blew us away.

00:51:03.050 --> 00:51:05.410
It just is like nectar into my mouth.

00:51:05.410 --> 00:51:06.920
But obviously you-
I just love it.

00:51:06.920 --> 00:51:08.370
Yeah, so "What We Do in the Shadows"

00:51:08.370 --> 00:51:12.607
is probably my favorite
television show comedy currently.

00:51:14.737 --> 00:51:16.410
But the specific thing,

00:51:16.410 --> 00:51:19.500
so Flight of the Concords
is the two-man band

00:51:20.830 --> 00:51:22.460
that Bret and Jemaine formed

00:51:23.530 --> 00:51:25.640
probably right around the
time that we were coming up

00:51:25.640 --> 00:51:27.170
and trying to get our comedy going.

00:51:27.170 --> 00:51:28.960
I specifically remember-
Early 2000s.

00:51:28.960 --> 00:51:30.540
I specifically remember-

00:51:30.540 --> 00:51:32.000
MySpace?

00:51:32.000 --> 00:51:36.330
Well, no, I remember being on a trip.

00:51:36.330 --> 00:51:37.163
Oh.

00:51:37.163 --> 00:51:39.640
A Campus Crusade for Christ
trip that we were going on,

00:51:39.640 --> 00:51:41.050
like probably 2002, 2003,

00:51:41.050 --> 00:51:46.050
where we're traveling around
doing our little comedy show

00:51:46.090 --> 00:51:48.470
that's really just an
evangelism training seminar

00:51:48.470 --> 00:51:49.560
for Christian students.

00:51:49.560 --> 00:51:51.438
But we have funny songs that turns out-

00:51:51.438 --> 00:51:52.271
We had a lot of funny songs.

00:51:52.271 --> 00:51:54.270
Weren't nearly as funny as these two guys.

00:51:54.270 --> 00:51:56.253
Well, and I remember
being in a hotel room.

00:51:56.253 --> 00:51:57.290
Yes, I remember this.

00:51:57.290 --> 00:51:59.240
Turning on HBO,

00:51:59.240 --> 00:52:03.050
and all of a sudden there
was these two dudes singing.

00:52:03.050 --> 00:52:04.280
It's a standup special,

00:52:04.280 --> 00:52:06.777
but it's two guys on a stage
and they're singing songs.

00:52:06.777 --> 00:52:09.270
"Business Time" is probably the first one,

00:52:09.270 --> 00:52:11.620
which is a song about having married sex

00:52:11.620 --> 00:52:13.680
and it's incredible.

00:52:13.680 --> 00:52:15.436
And I was just

00:52:15.436 --> 00:52:16.727
immediately like-
Married sex.

00:52:17.807 --> 00:52:21.250
"Oh gosh, these guys are
so much funnier than us."

00:52:21.250 --> 00:52:22.864
Like I was shot in the gut.

00:52:22.864 --> 00:52:25.360
Yeah, this has happened
so many times in my life

00:52:25.360 --> 00:52:27.680
where I feel like I'm doing something

00:52:27.680 --> 00:52:29.560
and then I see somebody
who's actually doing

00:52:29.560 --> 00:52:30.900
what I've think I'm doing.

00:52:30.900 --> 00:52:31.960
And then I see it happening,

00:52:31.960 --> 00:52:33.730
I'm like, "Damn it, I'm not doing it.

00:52:33.730 --> 00:52:35.760
I'm not actually that good at this."

00:52:35.760 --> 00:52:39.060
Yes, when we did, we look at each other

00:52:39.060 --> 00:52:42.030
and almost thought we had to turn it off.

00:52:42.030 --> 00:52:45.200
We didn't turn it off. We
watched the whole thing and then-

00:52:45.200 --> 00:52:46.203
I hated them. I loved hating them.

00:52:46.203 --> 00:52:47.360
We just couldn't talk,

00:52:47.360 --> 00:52:49.740
we couldn't talk about it
'cause they were so good,

00:52:49.740 --> 00:52:54.120
but it was so, so it wasn't
inspiring, it was challenging.

00:52:54.120 --> 00:52:54.953
It was infuriating.

00:52:54.953 --> 00:52:56.760
It was sobering.

00:52:56.760 --> 00:52:59.900
Yeah, and I would say that
every effort that we've made

00:52:59.900 --> 00:53:01.920
in musical comedy over the years

00:53:01.920 --> 00:53:06.340
has been an effort to
emulate them on some level

00:53:06.340 --> 00:53:09.573
without completely biting what
they're doing, what they did.

00:53:11.043 --> 00:53:14.940
Oh yeah, every line has a comedic effect.

00:53:14.940 --> 00:53:19.170
There's no filler and they
each have a character.

00:53:19.170 --> 00:53:22.130
And so we were students of what they did

00:53:22.130 --> 00:53:24.910
and this was all before
the HBO series came out

00:53:24.910 --> 00:53:27.330
that was an adaptation
of their first album.

00:53:27.330 --> 00:53:28.960
Yeah, which had like a music
video. It's the reason-

00:53:28.960 --> 00:53:31.380
It's obviously influenced "Buddy System"

00:53:31.380 --> 00:53:32.213
and that there's music videos-
It's the reason

00:53:32.213 --> 00:53:33.610
that "Buddy System" has a music video

00:53:33.610 --> 00:53:34.690
in every single episode.

00:53:34.690 --> 00:53:37.670
But the thing I'll say is-

00:53:37.670 --> 00:53:42.670
We actually went in a, we
calculated our comedy songs

00:53:43.290 --> 00:53:46.160
like as we were an audience on YouTube.

00:53:46.160 --> 00:53:47.210
As visuals.

00:53:48.710 --> 00:53:52.760
That, and we specifically talked about

00:53:52.760 --> 00:53:56.600
how not to be compared to them
by doing things differently

00:53:56.600 --> 00:53:58.358
so we wouldn't just seem
like a hack rip off.

00:53:58.358 --> 00:54:01.220
Like basically just avoiding like guitar,

00:54:01.220 --> 00:54:04.483
funky white boy music kind of thing.

00:54:06.190 --> 00:54:08.340
Or Kiwi accents.

00:54:08.340 --> 00:54:09.303
Yeah, yeah.

00:54:11.800 --> 00:54:13.870
I have a recurring dream of meeting one

00:54:13.870 --> 00:54:15.420
or both of them over the years

00:54:15.420 --> 00:54:18.700
and it's like it's an anxiety dream.

00:54:18.700 --> 00:54:21.067
Oh yeah. Because you know
they don't know who we are.

00:54:23.684 --> 00:54:25.460
But the thing is is that

00:54:28.180 --> 00:54:29.750
when I listen to our music,

00:54:29.750 --> 00:54:32.310
and again, we don't do a
lot of comedy music anymore.

00:54:32.310 --> 00:54:35.163
It's like we can only have,
there's only two of us.

00:54:38.010 --> 00:54:39.340
And they don't do it anymore really.

00:54:39.340 --> 00:54:40.480
I mean, they're doing it individually,

00:54:40.480 --> 00:54:43.560
but when I listen to our music,

00:54:43.560 --> 00:54:46.467
anything that we've created,
I always find fault with it.

00:54:46.467 --> 00:54:48.180
And this is any artist I guess.

00:54:48.180 --> 00:54:51.537
But like I listen to
our music and I'm like,

00:54:51.537 --> 00:54:54.750
"Yeah, well, I'd love to
rewrite that right now.

00:54:54.750 --> 00:54:57.490
Oh, I'd love to make that actually funny."

00:54:57.490 --> 00:54:59.700
But I always, and in my mind,

00:54:59.700 --> 00:55:02.223
I'm never not comparing
it to what they did.

00:55:03.170 --> 00:55:06.770
The moment of discovery of that special

00:55:06.770 --> 00:55:08.913
with like no point of reference at all.

00:55:10.180 --> 00:55:12.270
We turned it on.
It just was on HBO.

00:55:12.270 --> 00:55:14.410
Two guys sitting on stools, playing music,

00:55:14.410 --> 00:55:17.163
doing the exact same thing we were doing.

00:55:18.241 --> 00:55:20.350
I was like, "Whoa, whoa, whoa,
whoa. What's happening here?"

00:55:20.350 --> 00:55:21.703
It was really surreal.

00:55:25.000 --> 00:55:29.040
My number six is David Letterman.

00:55:29.040 --> 00:55:30.130
No.

00:55:30.130 --> 00:55:32.530
So what number did you
put David Letterman?

00:55:32.530 --> 00:55:34.970
'Cause I know you put
Letterman on your list.

00:55:34.970 --> 00:55:36.540
Number one.

00:55:36.540 --> 00:55:38.420
Oh, I stole your number one.

00:55:38.420 --> 00:55:40.408
I knew this was gonna happen.

00:55:40.408 --> 00:55:41.830
I knew he would be on your list

00:55:41.830 --> 00:55:43.863
and I knew that he wouldn't be number one.

00:55:45.070 --> 00:55:46.290
All right, say what you will.

00:55:46.290 --> 00:55:48.000
I think he's more of an,

00:55:48.000 --> 00:55:50.870
I think you watched more Letterman than me

00:55:50.870 --> 00:55:52.770
in you stayed up later than I did.

00:55:52.770 --> 00:55:54.780
Well that I still do.

00:55:54.780 --> 00:55:56.900
And you watched, so you
watched more Letterman.

00:55:56.900 --> 00:55:58.850
Okay, so again, this was a struggle

00:55:58.850 --> 00:56:01.540
because David Letterman is not an artist

00:56:01.540 --> 00:56:03.310
in the strict sense, right?

00:56:03.310 --> 00:56:07.083
He's a late-night show host,

00:56:08.090 --> 00:56:09.850
but this for me is kind of embracing-

00:56:09.850 --> 00:56:10.683
I don't know,

00:56:10.683 --> 00:56:12.300
I disagree with what you
just said, but go ahead.

00:56:12.300 --> 00:56:14.330
Well, no, no, I'm saying that

00:56:14.330 --> 00:56:16.360
I do believe that it's an art,

00:56:16.360 --> 00:56:17.193
but what I'm saying is

00:56:17.193 --> 00:56:19.700
that it's not a traditional
artist category.

00:56:19.700 --> 00:56:21.890
It's not a musician. It's not a filmmaker.

00:56:21.890 --> 00:56:24.620
It's not a TV show
runner. It's not a writer.

00:56:24.620 --> 00:56:27.720
It's a guy, it's a personality.

00:56:27.720 --> 00:56:31.350
But the reason that he's my number one

00:56:31.350 --> 00:56:33.530
is because in the same way that my dad

00:56:33.530 --> 00:56:38.170
sort of serves as this
foundational influence

00:56:38.170 --> 00:56:40.760
as the things that I find funny

00:56:40.760 --> 00:56:44.120
and the way that I end
up being funny naturally

00:56:44.120 --> 00:56:46.890
is very influenced by
the disposition of a guy

00:56:46.890 --> 00:56:48.010
like David Letterman.

00:56:48.010 --> 00:56:49.940
I've actually come off of that quite a bit

00:56:49.940 --> 00:56:52.110
in the past probably five or six years,

00:56:52.110 --> 00:56:54.820
just as people's tastes
in comedy has changed.

00:56:54.820 --> 00:56:59.720
So the dry, he had a dry delivery

00:56:59.720 --> 00:57:03.210
and it was kind of a
knowing slyness to it.

00:57:03.210 --> 00:57:05.740
Well, and most people, if you're younger

00:57:05.740 --> 00:57:09.030
and your only experience with
Letterman is the CBS days,

00:57:09.030 --> 00:57:12.100
and specifically like the
last three to four years-

00:57:12.100 --> 00:57:13.747
Grumpy Letterman.
You would just be like,

00:57:13.747 --> 00:57:15.710
"This is a angry guy."

00:57:15.710 --> 00:57:18.270
This is a guy who's not
happy, who's kind of mean,

00:57:18.270 --> 00:57:21.170
doesn't seem to like anyone,
doesn't seem to like himself.

00:57:22.220 --> 00:57:24.270
I think that's how the younger
generation responds to it,

00:57:24.270 --> 00:57:26.690
and if you look at some of
the way that I kind of behaved

00:57:26.690 --> 00:57:27.810
in some of our earlier content,

00:57:27.810 --> 00:57:29.400
you might come to the same conclusion.

00:57:29.400 --> 00:57:30.770
It was a calculated thing.

00:57:30.770 --> 00:57:33.470
I was like this is the
role that I'm playing.

00:57:33.470 --> 00:57:35.770
And as I realized that a lot of people

00:57:35.770 --> 00:57:38.280
who enjoy internet
content are not into that.

00:57:38.280 --> 00:57:39.920
They don't want to see somebody,

00:57:39.920 --> 00:57:41.810
they don't like meanness

00:57:41.810 --> 00:57:44.600
and they don't even really
like slyness as much.

00:57:44.600 --> 00:57:47.730
So I'm a lot more jovial of a guy

00:57:47.730 --> 00:57:49.930
in terms of my comedy at this point.

00:57:49.930 --> 00:57:52.253
But if you go back to his earlier stuff.

00:57:53.720 --> 00:57:55.710
Well, he was kinda like
that. He was that way.

00:57:55.710 --> 00:57:57.232
But the reason he's my number one-

00:57:57.232 --> 00:58:01.410
He was smilier and he was silly too.

00:58:01.410 --> 00:58:02.414
Super silly, and it wasn't-

00:58:02.414 --> 00:58:04.820
It was a strange
combination. Silly and dry.

00:58:04.820 --> 00:58:06.800
Yeah, well the reason he's my number one

00:58:06.800 --> 00:58:10.830
is because for better or worse,

00:58:10.830 --> 00:58:13.490
the thing that we are,

00:58:13.490 --> 00:58:15.580
and I am most known for

00:58:15.580 --> 00:58:18.770
is being one of the two hosts
of "Good Mythical Morning."

00:58:18.770 --> 00:58:21.460
Like again, at the end of my life,

00:58:21.460 --> 00:58:23.430
the thing that will
have had the most impact

00:58:23.430 --> 00:58:25.660
in the entertainment world
will be all these episodes

00:58:25.660 --> 00:58:27.850
of "Good Mythical Morning" that I've made.

00:58:27.850 --> 00:58:32.850
And I approach

00:58:33.140 --> 00:58:34.607
the way that we think about that show

00:58:34.607 --> 00:58:36.900
and the way that we embrace

00:58:36.900 --> 00:58:41.420
both some kind of structure
that is presentational,

00:58:41.420 --> 00:58:43.440
that feels kind of traditional,

00:58:43.440 --> 00:58:45.940
that feels like a television show

00:58:46.950 --> 00:58:49.603
in a way that a lot of
YouTube content doesn't feel.

00:58:50.760 --> 00:58:53.380
That's a huge part of "Good
Mythical Morning's" consistency,

00:58:53.380 --> 00:58:55.800
reliability, and sort of point of view.

00:58:55.800 --> 00:58:58.010
But the other piece of it is

00:58:58.010 --> 00:59:02.220
this unhinged completely

00:59:06.241 --> 00:59:10.420
just silliness, just
a embrace of silliness

00:59:10.420 --> 00:59:13.742
to the point that it gets weird.

00:59:13.742 --> 00:59:16.560
He did a segment called Will It Float

00:59:16.560 --> 00:59:18.230
and it was just taking an object-

00:59:18.230 --> 00:59:19.130
Yeah, he did.

00:59:19.130 --> 00:59:23.040
Putting it in water and then describing

00:59:23.040 --> 00:59:24.370
if it was floating or not.

00:59:24.370 --> 00:59:27.760
Yeah, and he incorporated
his mom into his content.

00:59:27.760 --> 00:59:30.120
The reason that we have our parents

00:59:30.120 --> 00:59:31.603
in our content in that way,

00:59:32.553 --> 00:59:35.063
he would call his mom and
talk to her on the phone.

00:59:37.970 --> 00:59:39.960
He did these, if you go
back and look at the bits,

00:59:39.960 --> 00:59:41.920
now there's a really good book about this.

00:59:41.920 --> 00:59:46.300
Yeah, can you Google
the Letterman biography?

00:59:46.300 --> 00:59:48.970
'Cause we read it. We
got Stevie to read it.

00:59:48.970 --> 00:59:50.570
It was like, I mean,

00:59:50.570 --> 00:59:54.500
it reminded us stuff I
had actually never seen,

00:59:54.500 --> 00:59:57.000
but like the ethos of
"Good Mythical Morning"

00:59:57.000 --> 01:00:00.340
is much more like the early

01:00:01.570 --> 01:00:04.020
and successful Letterman "Late Show"

01:00:04.020 --> 01:00:05.610
than it is "Wayne's World."

01:00:05.610 --> 01:00:06.637
I will say that.
Oh yeah.

01:00:06.637 --> 01:00:08.180
"Wayne's World" not on our list.

01:00:08.180 --> 01:00:09.183
Oh yeah, yeah.

01:00:11.030 --> 01:00:13.770
And you kind of fell in more
of that Letterman thing.

01:00:13.770 --> 01:00:15.170
Just tell us when you get the...

01:00:15.170 --> 01:00:20.170
But for me, I think of you
as more of a Letterman type

01:00:22.040 --> 01:00:27.040
and then I kinda like
my comedic sensibility,

01:00:27.420 --> 01:00:29.830
I don't know, I don't know who that is.

01:00:29.830 --> 01:00:33.750
I think I can see more of
Letterman and your dad in you.

01:00:33.750 --> 01:00:35.540
I actually have a hard time seeing,

01:00:35.540 --> 01:00:37.150
and I don't have anybody on my list

01:00:37.150 --> 01:00:39.163
that's like that version from me.

01:00:40.110 --> 01:00:41.433
Don't say Paul Shaffer.

01:00:43.683 --> 01:00:45.890
Hey, that was a joke. I
knew you wouldn't say that.

01:00:45.890 --> 01:00:47.453
Don't say Don Knotts.

01:00:48.300 --> 01:00:49.300
I mean, I do think

01:00:50.442 --> 01:00:54.840
that like Dana Carvey is a type,

01:00:54.840 --> 01:00:57.020
not just because you look like him,

01:00:57.020 --> 01:00:58.910
but I think there is a...

01:01:01.070 --> 01:01:01.903
Is that it?

01:01:01.903 --> 01:01:03.710
Yeah, Letterman the last guard of-

01:01:03.710 --> 01:01:04.843
The last giant.
Last giant.

01:01:04.843 --> 01:01:06.933
"The Last Giant of Late Night."

01:01:06.933 --> 01:01:11.240
And if I recall, this book
is also pretty honest.

01:01:11.240 --> 01:01:13.360
He's a complex character.

01:01:13.360 --> 01:01:15.690
He did a lot of things that he regrets,

01:01:15.690 --> 01:01:18.070
that obviously I do not
endorse everything that he did

01:01:18.070 --> 01:01:21.160
as a person or even everything
that he did comedically.

01:01:21.160 --> 01:01:23.250
But I watched so much Letterman

01:01:23.250 --> 01:01:26.618
with Ben Greenwood growing
up, Ben Greenwood and I.

01:01:26.618 --> 01:01:28.470
And Ben Greenwood, again,

01:01:28.470 --> 01:01:30.630
I guess in some senses
Ben should be on the list

01:01:30.630 --> 01:01:34.670
because Ben was the one that
was really into late-night TV.

01:01:34.670 --> 01:01:36.300
My dad was not into late-night TV.

01:01:36.300 --> 01:01:38.390
My dad was not into that kind of comedy.

01:01:38.390 --> 01:01:40.280
He also didn't stay up that late.

01:01:40.280 --> 01:01:42.790
But Ben would be like, "We're
gonna watch Johnny Carson."

01:01:42.790 --> 01:01:44.640
And he's like, "Oh,
and when we really want

01:01:44.640 --> 01:01:46.660
something really silly,
we're gonna watch Letterman."

01:01:46.660 --> 01:01:48.730
And we watched Letterman on NBC.

01:01:48.730 --> 01:01:50.457
The dude was sitting
there with his curly hair.

01:01:50.457 --> 01:01:52.430
"The Late Late Show" or
whatever it was called.

01:01:52.430 --> 01:01:53.660
Tennis shoes.

01:01:53.660 --> 01:01:54.850
And he would just do,

01:01:54.850 --> 01:01:59.043
watching an adult do such stupid stuff,

01:02:00.050 --> 01:02:01.597
it's really made us who we are.

01:02:01.597 --> 01:02:04.100
And the parallels, yeah, from the book,

01:02:04.100 --> 01:02:07.560
he was a weatherman and he like had this,

01:02:07.560 --> 01:02:11.130
and then he moved into
being a comedian and a host.

01:02:11.130 --> 01:02:13.123
He took a circuitous
path to entertainment.

01:02:13.123 --> 01:02:17.520
So just like we did, he had
an interesting relationship.

01:02:17.520 --> 01:02:22.520
Well, he had a relationship
with his producer,

01:02:22.800 --> 01:02:25.063
like they were together for years.

01:02:26.480 --> 01:02:28.440
And she's very instrumental
in the book too.

01:02:28.440 --> 01:02:29.950
So it's like, that's an interesting thing

01:02:29.950 --> 01:02:33.633
that there's like a Stevie
as producer kind of thing.

01:02:33.633 --> 01:02:35.070
But not as a relationship.

01:02:35.070 --> 01:02:36.650
Not as a relationship though.

01:02:36.650 --> 01:02:39.020
But like, there were an entire episodes

01:02:39.020 --> 01:02:42.200
where she would trick him.

01:02:42.200 --> 01:02:43.790
Like, it would be a joke on Letterman.

01:02:43.790 --> 01:02:44.735
Like he wouldn't-

01:02:44.735 --> 01:02:45.568
He would get mad.

01:02:45.568 --> 01:02:50.000
Yeah, he would get mad, but
it would be live to tape, so.

01:02:50.000 --> 01:02:52.770
There's a lot of stories there
that were like we related to,

01:02:52.770 --> 01:02:55.300
and that make that very formative.

01:02:55.300 --> 01:02:57.760
So I don't know if I
accepted Dana Carvey or not.

01:02:57.760 --> 01:03:00.740
I know I look like him and there's a tone,

01:03:00.740 --> 01:03:02.460
but he's a character actor, so.

01:03:02.460 --> 01:03:04.913
I think I'm just talking about the-

01:03:07.530 --> 01:03:08.660
He never played himself.

01:03:08.660 --> 01:03:12.250
You're not gonna like
the word zany, right?

01:03:12.250 --> 01:03:13.870
You don't like the word zany.

01:03:13.870 --> 01:03:16.300
But if you're like, "Okay,
this guy or this guy,

01:03:16.300 --> 01:03:18.088
who's the zanier one?"
Well, it's like-

01:03:18.088 --> 01:03:18.921
Well, you know what I'm saying?

01:03:18.921 --> 01:03:19.754
People are gonna pick you.

01:03:19.754 --> 01:03:23.500
If there's the dry and
then if the opposite's wet,

01:03:23.500 --> 01:03:26.307
it's like, "Okay, I'm the
wet guy, you're the dry guy."

01:03:30.415 --> 01:03:33.617
Yeah, but I don't know
exactly. I'm curious.

01:03:35.736 --> 01:03:40.127
You can #EarBiscuits,
let us both know who else

01:03:41.680 --> 01:03:42.513
if you're a student of comedy.

01:03:42.513 --> 01:03:43.346
I think there's a big-

01:03:43.346 --> 01:03:44.950
Beyond just who I look like.

01:03:44.950 --> 01:03:45.970
There's a distinction.

01:03:45.970 --> 01:03:47.150
And who Rhett reminds you of style wise.

01:03:47.150 --> 01:03:50.080
Again, this is an oversimplification,

01:03:50.080 --> 01:03:51.920
but the thing that we say quite a bit

01:03:51.920 --> 01:03:55.720
is that I'm usually funny on purpose

01:03:55.720 --> 01:03:57.560
and you're usually funny on accident.

01:03:57.560 --> 01:03:59.740
Now that's an oversimplification

01:03:59.740 --> 01:04:04.740
because a lot of your
accidental humor is purposeful.

01:04:04.810 --> 01:04:06.110
It's a different mode.

01:04:06.110 --> 01:04:09.580
You don't know what's gonna
come outta your mouth.

01:04:09.580 --> 01:04:11.210
You don't know exactly
what's gonna happen,

01:04:11.210 --> 01:04:14.470
but you go into a sort of
like a trance-like state.

01:04:14.470 --> 01:04:15.530
I mean, "Good Mythical Evening"

01:04:15.530 --> 01:04:18.260
obviously is the perfect example of this.

01:04:18.260 --> 01:04:19.093
But it's like-

01:04:19.093 --> 01:04:21.510
It wasn't a trance, but yeah.

01:04:21.510 --> 01:04:23.170
It's more chaotic.

01:04:23.170 --> 01:04:24.650
Yeah.

01:04:24.650 --> 01:04:27.350
It's chaotic, which is great.

01:04:27.350 --> 01:04:29.380
And that makes me think of a Martin Short,

01:04:29.380 --> 01:04:31.430
but I didn't watch a lot of Martin Short.

01:04:32.770 --> 01:04:36.970
So it's not, I'm not
actively emulating somebody

01:04:36.970 --> 01:04:37.810
and I can't identify them.

01:04:37.810 --> 01:04:38.890
But actually that's really good

01:04:38.890 --> 01:04:41.460
'cause if you watch "Only
Murders in the Building,"

01:04:41.460 --> 01:04:42.300
which I did, and I enjoyed it.

01:04:42.300 --> 01:04:44.250
It's not my favorite, but I enjoyed it.

01:04:45.240 --> 01:04:47.650
I am the Steve Martin.
You are the Martin Short.

01:04:47.650 --> 01:04:51.448
All day long, without a doubt.

01:04:51.448 --> 01:04:54.730
If you have to put us into
that duo, that's how it tracks.

01:04:54.730 --> 01:04:58.163
I think that works.
Yeah, I think that works.

01:04:59.350 --> 01:05:01.890
But we're still open to
others, #EarBiscuits.

01:05:03.470 --> 01:05:05.420
Sorry to steal your number one.

01:05:05.420 --> 01:05:08.060
Yeah, that hurts. Just like that playlist.

01:05:08.060 --> 01:05:11.390
My number, so now we need your number six,

01:05:11.390 --> 01:05:12.240
no, your number five.

01:05:12.240 --> 01:05:13.200
My number five,

01:05:13.200 --> 01:05:15.560
you already stole that
as well, Ross McElwee.

01:05:15.560 --> 01:05:19.377
Okay, my number five is "Seinfeld."

01:05:20.280 --> 01:05:21.290
That's my number two.

01:05:21.290 --> 01:05:23.103
Oh God. Okay.

01:05:24.300 --> 01:05:26.200
Yeah, and I'll just-

01:05:26.200 --> 01:05:27.160
I'm really interested

01:05:27.160 --> 01:05:29.590
what your top three are gonna be now.

01:05:29.590 --> 01:05:31.420
For "Seinfeld," I just boiled it down

01:05:31.420 --> 01:05:35.170
to like the relatable observational humor

01:05:35.170 --> 01:05:39.420
is something that became
a cornerstone of how-

01:05:39.420 --> 01:05:40.253
Anything we write.

01:05:40.253 --> 01:05:43.710
What we would build to connect on YouTube.

01:05:43.710 --> 01:05:45.220
But it was instinctual

01:05:45.220 --> 01:05:48.120
because we were so
obsessed with that show.

01:05:48.120 --> 01:05:51.230
I actually put "Seinfeld"
slash Larry David

01:05:51.230 --> 01:05:52.830
as one thing, right?

01:05:52.830 --> 01:05:54.420
Yeah, yeah, you got to.

01:05:54.420 --> 01:05:57.520
'Cause Larry David was the
one that was really fueling

01:05:57.520 --> 01:05:59.310
the writing of all that stuff.

01:05:59.310 --> 01:06:01.620
I mean, obviously
"Seinfeld" was very much,

01:06:01.620 --> 01:06:02.720
it was coming from his standup,

01:06:02.720 --> 01:06:04.167
which was super observational.

01:06:04.167 --> 01:06:06.280
"What's the deal with this?"

01:06:06.280 --> 01:06:08.160
But bringing it into the real world

01:06:08.160 --> 01:06:09.980
and into a real conversation,

01:06:09.980 --> 01:06:12.343
Larry David is a genius at
that and obviously he does it,

01:06:12.343 --> 01:06:14.290
it's on display in "Curb Your Enthusiasm,"

01:06:14.290 --> 01:06:15.340
which I love as well.

01:06:17.100 --> 01:06:18.730
I think the thing that-

01:06:18.730 --> 01:06:21.500
He's the George Costanza
character in "Seinfeld."

01:06:21.500 --> 01:06:26.500
The thing that it so resonates
with me as a writer, right?

01:06:27.320 --> 01:06:30.250
And that's why I put them at number two

01:06:30.250 --> 01:06:33.390
because if I start thinking about,

01:06:33.390 --> 01:06:35.320
okay, you got these two
people in this scene

01:06:35.320 --> 01:06:38.580
and we need to accomplish this plot wise,

01:06:38.580 --> 01:06:40.890
but they need to get there
in an entertaining way,

01:06:40.890 --> 01:06:43.690
so they need to be having
a conversation about this.

01:06:43.690 --> 01:06:48.690
Well, the way that Larry David
sees conversational humor

01:06:49.600 --> 01:06:51.590
is it's the same thing that,

01:06:51.590 --> 01:06:53.540
it comes from this tendency
that's in both of us,

01:06:53.540 --> 01:06:56.810
which is, hey, we just hung out with,

01:06:56.810 --> 01:06:58.240
you were just talking about
it on the last podcast.

01:06:58.240 --> 01:07:00.930
We just hung out with this
guy. Let's talk about that hat.

01:07:00.930 --> 01:07:01.763
Let's pick him.

01:07:01.763 --> 01:07:04.080
Let's talk about the hat he had on.

01:07:04.080 --> 01:07:06.130
Look, did you see what he was doing?

01:07:06.130 --> 01:07:07.310
Did you see what he was doing

01:07:07.310 --> 01:07:09.136
with his left hand and his nose?

01:07:09.136 --> 01:07:10.300
Explain that to me.

01:07:10.300 --> 01:07:13.010
Right, and it's nothing in "Seinfeld."

01:07:13.010 --> 01:07:15.910
There were no jokes. It
was not joke oriented.

01:07:15.910 --> 01:07:20.100
It was conceptual, conversational.

01:07:20.100 --> 01:07:24.410
And so it very much is
how we like to write.

01:07:24.410 --> 01:07:27.970
And it gets very specific and it gets,

01:07:27.970 --> 01:07:29.763
it's like, oh, you
typically wouldn't think

01:07:29.763 --> 01:07:32.270
that we should be having
a conversation about this

01:07:32.270 --> 01:07:34.663
or this is where the humor
should be coming from.

01:07:35.580 --> 01:07:36.430
It feels like

01:07:37.660 --> 01:07:39.910
I don't even know how to
articulate it very well.

01:07:39.910 --> 01:07:42.607
But when I watch

01:07:42.607 --> 01:07:45.340
and the only way I really
watch "Curb Your Enthusiasm"

01:07:45.340 --> 01:07:48.220
these days is like on TikTok.

01:07:48.220 --> 01:07:50.043
I hate to say it.

01:07:50.043 --> 01:07:52.000
Oh, I gotta get in on that.

01:07:52.000 --> 01:07:53.070
A clip will just come up,

01:07:53.070 --> 01:07:55.650
whether it's on the "Curb Your
Enthusiasm" actual account

01:07:55.650 --> 01:07:57.060
or just somebody has reposted it.

01:07:57.060 --> 01:07:59.540
And I'm just like this moment
where something happens

01:07:59.540 --> 01:08:02.720
and I'm just like, "Ah man,
this is like, that's so great."

01:08:02.720 --> 01:08:04.010
So that's your number two?

01:08:04.010 --> 01:08:05.780
Yeah, you've taken my one and my two.

01:08:05.780 --> 01:08:06.793
What's your four?

01:08:08.520 --> 01:08:09.720
Coen brothers.

01:08:10.790 --> 01:08:11.960
What we were just talking about

01:08:11.960 --> 01:08:15.330
made me realize that they
weren't on my list, but yeah.

01:08:15.330 --> 01:08:17.500
The Coen brothers are not on your list?

01:08:17.500 --> 01:08:18.520
I just forgot.

01:08:18.520 --> 01:08:21.040
I just forgot. But yeah,
it's that same thing.

01:08:21.040 --> 01:08:26.040
It's a different, it's
character driven comedy,

01:08:26.320 --> 01:08:27.930
which we've talked about before

01:08:27.930 --> 01:08:30.020
is another big part of like what,

01:08:30.020 --> 01:08:33.080
finding ourselves really
loving their comedy

01:08:33.080 --> 01:08:34.630
and then trying to figure out why

01:08:34.630 --> 01:08:36.360
and having conversations about it.

01:08:36.360 --> 01:08:39.890
There's so much about the
Coen brothers I can say.

01:08:39.890 --> 01:08:41.853
One, they're a partnership, right?

01:08:42.980 --> 01:08:46.113
Which is obviously very relatable to us.

01:08:49.790 --> 01:08:51.530
There's a creative evolution

01:08:51.530 --> 01:08:52.880
over the course of their career

01:08:52.880 --> 01:08:55.600
of them like trying something

01:08:55.600 --> 01:08:58.570
and you see these like glimmers
of it in like "Blood Simple"

01:08:58.570 --> 01:08:59.980
in which we would like,

01:08:59.980 --> 01:09:01.580
we kind of went into
our like Coen brothers

01:09:01.580 --> 01:09:04.697
like film study, kind
of our version of that.

01:09:04.697 --> 01:09:07.240
"Let's go watch 'Blood Simple'
and make ourselves like it."

01:09:07.240 --> 01:09:08.073
Right?

01:09:10.900 --> 01:09:15.770
And then you get what I
think is the, just for me,

01:09:15.770 --> 01:09:19.360
maybe for you is just
quintessential perfect expression

01:09:19.360 --> 01:09:21.210
of them as filmmakers is "O Brother."

01:09:22.360 --> 01:09:26.773
That's where it gets into
this thing that is the humor,

01:09:27.670 --> 01:09:30.350
the music, the characters.

01:09:30.350 --> 01:09:33.340
Everything just lines up
in a way that they're,

01:09:33.340 --> 01:09:34.900
and they basically just
hit it out of the park

01:09:34.900 --> 01:09:36.660
every time now as far as I'm concerned.

01:09:36.660 --> 01:09:37.960
I mean, there's a couple, couple times

01:09:37.960 --> 01:09:39.700
where I'm not quite is
into the subject matter,

01:09:39.700 --> 01:09:41.263
I don't quite relate as much.

01:09:42.650 --> 01:09:45.860
We talked about this on
the our top 10 films.

01:09:45.860 --> 01:09:48.313
It's kind of there's that overlap there.

01:09:50.724 --> 01:09:55.050
They're so aspirational
because what they do it,

01:09:55.050 --> 01:09:56.660
they do so well.

01:09:56.660 --> 01:09:58.910
And I think, again,

01:09:58.910 --> 01:10:01.270
underneath everything
that we've accomplished

01:10:01.270 --> 01:10:04.753
has always been this
desire to make movies.

01:10:06.300 --> 01:10:10.053
And I still wanna do it, and
yes, we have written one.

01:10:12.580 --> 01:10:14.520
And I just don't think
I'm ever gonna let go

01:10:14.520 --> 01:10:16.410
of that desire to be a filmmaker,

01:10:16.410 --> 01:10:19.120
not instead of, or in place of what we do,

01:10:19.120 --> 01:10:20.440
but in an addition to what we do,

01:10:20.440 --> 01:10:22.240
because there's just something about

01:10:23.610 --> 01:10:26.540
the way that you can bring
all this creative energy

01:10:26.540 --> 01:10:31.540
into a time and place over the
course of a couple of hours

01:10:32.210 --> 01:10:35.890
and you can get it done
in a year or whatever

01:10:35.890 --> 01:10:37.700
from start to finish.

01:10:37.700 --> 01:10:40.090
There's just something so
attractive about that process.

01:10:40.090 --> 01:10:42.760
And when I think about
how that's ultimately

01:10:42.760 --> 01:10:45.580
sort of the white whale for me personally

01:10:45.580 --> 01:10:47.610
of like that that's last
one I'm always chasing

01:10:47.610 --> 01:10:49.093
is I wanna make that movie.

01:10:50.060 --> 01:10:53.323
They are the embodiment of it
having been done perfectly.

01:10:54.900 --> 01:10:57.810
My number four wouldn't
have been on my list,

01:10:57.810 --> 01:11:00.639
except that like in a recent conversation,

01:11:00.639 --> 01:11:02.393
I was reminded of them.

01:11:03.970 --> 01:11:05.460
John Boy &amp; Billy Big Show.

01:11:05.460 --> 01:11:07.140
Oh wow!

01:11:07.140 --> 01:11:09.150
I thought about them for
a second, and yeah, yeah.

01:11:09.150 --> 01:11:10.860
Well, because-
I didn't put them on.

01:11:10.860 --> 01:11:12.840
You know like in high school

01:11:14.070 --> 01:11:15.730
we were listening to their morning show,

01:11:15.730 --> 01:11:17.693
still going to this day,

01:11:19.580 --> 01:11:24.050
but it was, they were a duo that was,

01:11:24.050 --> 01:11:25.410
they were radio personalities.

01:11:25.410 --> 01:11:26.810
So it was conversational

01:11:26.810 --> 01:11:29.830
and you'd be with them
for like, for summer,

01:11:29.830 --> 01:11:33.480
like on the job with my
dad like painting houses,

01:11:33.480 --> 01:11:34.720
we would listen to it in the truck.

01:11:34.720 --> 01:11:36.020
We'd listen to it while we were painting,

01:11:36.020 --> 01:11:39.640
I would listen to the
whole three or three hours

01:11:39.640 --> 01:11:41.580
every single weekday.

01:11:41.580 --> 01:11:44.656
And they'd come back
from commercial and say,

01:11:44.656 --> 01:11:46.640
"All right, we're gonna call so and so."

01:11:46.640 --> 01:11:48.960
And it would be the different,

01:11:48.960 --> 01:11:50.350
they would have sketches

01:11:50.350 --> 01:11:53.600
where it was like they were
playing different characters,

01:11:53.600 --> 01:11:57.300
but it's like you had this keen awareness.

01:11:57.300 --> 01:11:58.770
Well, I don't know if I knew at first,

01:11:58.770 --> 01:12:01.540
but eventually I wised up, I was like-

01:12:01.540 --> 01:12:02.373
They were playing them.

01:12:02.373 --> 01:12:04.130
These two guys are beating everybody.

01:12:04.130 --> 01:12:05.167
These people aren't calling in.

01:12:05.167 --> 01:12:07.200
I'd have comedians come in and guess,

01:12:07.200 --> 01:12:11.620
but they had their own world with,

01:12:11.620 --> 01:12:15.657
it was like, self-contained that was,

01:12:15.657 --> 01:12:17.563
and you became a part of it.

01:12:18.780 --> 01:12:21.580
And like you got to know them as people.

01:12:21.580 --> 01:12:24.870
And I think that we
have this understanding

01:12:24.870 --> 01:12:27.950
of the audience experience
for "Good Mythical Morning"

01:12:27.950 --> 01:12:28.893
and for this show,

01:12:29.818 --> 01:12:33.270
because we had that experience with them.

01:12:33.270 --> 01:12:35.850
That's why it was so thrilling
when they, like I said,

01:12:35.850 --> 01:12:37.950
they invited us to

01:12:37.950 --> 01:12:41.690
once we started making those viral videos-

01:12:41.690 --> 01:12:42.980
We got to be on the show.
The commercials,

01:12:42.980 --> 01:12:43.900
we got to be on the show.

01:12:43.900 --> 01:12:44.890
And I took my dad there

01:12:44.890 --> 01:12:49.730
and John Boy took us to his
ranch, his like farmhouse.

01:12:49.730 --> 01:12:53.050
And like, we hung out
and stayed the night.

01:12:53.050 --> 01:12:55.450
I actually got a text from Carl.

01:12:55.450 --> 01:12:56.460
Carl the cook?

01:12:56.460 --> 01:12:58.007
Yeah, he went to my old-

01:12:58.007 --> 01:12:59.520
The guy who made their barbecue sauce?

01:12:59.520 --> 01:13:01.360
Yeah, he said, "I'll send
y'all some barbecue sauce."

01:13:01.360 --> 01:13:02.397
The guy who got drunk and said,

01:13:02.397 --> 01:13:07.397
"Man, you has anybody ever told
you you look like a falcon?"

01:13:07.500 --> 01:13:09.080
You did, your face looked like a falcon.

01:13:09.080 --> 01:13:09.913
I couldn't figure out

01:13:09.913 --> 01:13:12.190
how to get in touch with him because-

01:13:12.190 --> 01:13:13.023
Falcon, man.

01:13:14.980 --> 01:13:16.438
It went to my like Google number

01:13:16.438 --> 01:13:17.707
and it's like it couldn't,

01:13:17.707 --> 01:13:19.443
but we gotta get in touch with him.

01:13:20.342 --> 01:13:21.340
I mean the more you talk about it,

01:13:21.340 --> 01:13:22.894
the more I realize-
But all character work

01:13:22.894 --> 01:13:23.727
and the sketch work.
So much of that influenced-

01:13:23.727 --> 01:13:26.475
And it was audio. It was
like what we did as kids.

01:13:26.475 --> 01:13:29.370
We would do radio shows
and it went on tapes.

01:13:29.370 --> 01:13:32.370
It really influenced a
lot of the characters

01:13:32.370 --> 01:13:34.110
that we created in the early days.

01:13:34.110 --> 01:13:36.330
The redneck character.
The redneck guys.

01:13:36.330 --> 01:13:39.763
Which had like all different,
we had an array of them.

01:13:41.657 --> 01:13:43.540
And when you talk about
just the amount of-

01:13:43.540 --> 01:13:44.590
Y'all keep them straight up there.

01:13:44.590 --> 01:13:48.500
Actual exposure to a potential influence.

01:13:48.500 --> 01:13:50.240
We just, I mean, you that's what you did.

01:13:50.240 --> 01:13:51.400
You turned on John Boy and Billy

01:13:51.400 --> 01:13:52.810
on the way to high
school every single day.

01:13:52.810 --> 01:13:57.380
Yeah, so it's like we
absorbed their sensibilities.

01:13:57.380 --> 01:13:59.810
And we got to know them
as real people too,

01:13:59.810 --> 01:14:01.663
so you saw both sides of it.

01:14:02.580 --> 01:14:04.970
All right, I think I've wiped you out.

01:14:04.970 --> 01:14:06.710
No, I have one more. My number three.

01:14:06.710 --> 01:14:07.910
All right, what's your number three?

01:14:07.910 --> 01:14:08.950
I think I know what,

01:14:08.950 --> 01:14:10.820
I know someone that's in your top three

01:14:10.820 --> 01:14:13.490
that is on my honorable mentions,
but I'll let you say it.

01:14:13.490 --> 01:14:15.300
But my number three is Weird Al.

01:14:15.300 --> 01:14:18.070
Okay, Weird Al is my number two.

01:14:18.070 --> 01:14:21.689
Okay, I mean, so you get
to talk about him first.

01:14:21.689 --> 01:14:22.960
I regret when Weird Al

01:14:22.960 --> 01:14:26.373
came on "Good Mythical
Morning" that we didn't like-

01:14:27.330 --> 01:14:29.250
Worship him. Worship him.

01:14:29.250 --> 01:14:30.870
Give him offerings.

01:14:30.870 --> 01:14:32.300
Give him some like a burn offering.

01:14:32.300 --> 01:14:37.030
The first cassette tape we
both ever owned was Weird Al

01:14:38.840 --> 01:14:40.430
and I still have mine.

01:14:40.430 --> 01:14:43.303
I wish I would've brought it
that day and had him sign it.

01:14:44.360 --> 01:14:46.500
We should have him back on the show.

01:14:46.500 --> 01:14:47.460
We should, we should.

01:14:47.460 --> 01:14:50.320
That was during GMM 22
where it was so hectic

01:14:50.320 --> 01:14:51.745
that it was like, "Oh, Weird Al's here?

01:14:51.745 --> 01:14:54.290
I don't really have time to process that."

01:14:54.290 --> 01:14:56.510
He's playing his accordion on the show.

01:14:56.510 --> 01:14:57.810
Yeah, but there's also like a woman

01:14:57.810 --> 01:14:58.830
who looks like a stripper.

01:14:58.830 --> 01:14:59.663
I, like, what?

01:14:59.663 --> 01:15:00.883
It was a strange episode.

01:15:00.883 --> 01:15:02.277
What was, I don't even-
It wasn't all about him

01:15:02.277 --> 01:15:06.230
and it was a disservice
to the great Weird Al,

01:15:06.230 --> 01:15:08.980
number two creative influence on my list.

01:15:08.980 --> 01:15:13.240
Because I got engaged

01:15:13.240 --> 01:15:15.330
with this comedy music

01:15:15.330 --> 01:15:18.550
and it just like made the most sense.

01:15:18.550 --> 01:15:23.020
Like I wasn't even a music
fan before that, really.

01:15:23.020 --> 01:15:26.530
Like I wasn't, because my
older stepsister liked music

01:15:26.530 --> 01:15:29.070
and I wasn't gonna like
anything she liked.

01:15:29.070 --> 01:15:30.960
Until Weird Al-
Brought you out of music.

01:15:30.960 --> 01:15:34.250
I was like, she is, I mean, he is so,

01:15:34.250 --> 01:15:37.023
this is, this just totally makes sense.

01:15:38.308 --> 01:15:40.807
It was like it was tailor
made, tailor made for us.

01:15:40.807 --> 01:15:42.390
And it's strange that like

01:15:43.820 --> 01:15:46.010
I didn't know on every one of his albums,

01:15:46.010 --> 01:15:49.970
I didn't watch his movie.

01:15:49.970 --> 01:15:53.320
So it wasn't like I was a super fan,

01:15:53.320 --> 01:15:56.740
but at such a formative time in our lives

01:15:56.740 --> 01:15:58.590
when we were like really hitting it off

01:15:58.590 --> 01:16:02.773
and like making those dumb
tapes that we were making.

01:16:04.440 --> 01:16:06.830
We weren't writing songs together yet,

01:16:06.830 --> 01:16:09.930
but it was just like that stuff,

01:16:09.930 --> 01:16:13.970
it's foundational in terms of comedy

01:16:13.970 --> 01:16:15.870
and obviously starting our career

01:16:15.870 --> 01:16:19.010
as musical comedians for the most part.

01:16:19.010 --> 01:16:22.410
And one of the first things really

01:16:23.490 --> 01:16:27.060
apart from some of the things
that we did in high school,

01:16:27.060 --> 01:16:29.990
and then a couple of the
videos that we made in college,

01:16:29.990 --> 01:16:34.294
the first thing that we did
together as a comedic duo

01:16:34.294 --> 01:16:35.763
was comedy music.

01:16:37.080 --> 01:16:40.020
It was those first couple
of Christmas conferences.

01:16:40.020 --> 01:16:43.180
Oh no, I'm talking about like,

01:16:43.180 --> 01:16:46.160
you're down with Halloween,
like rewriting the words.

01:16:46.160 --> 01:16:47.060
Yeah, yeah, that's true, that's true.

01:16:47.060 --> 01:16:49.460
Rewriting the words
and getting up on stage

01:16:49.460 --> 01:16:50.293
at the fall festival.

01:16:50.293 --> 01:16:51.600
But anytime that we were performing.

01:16:51.600 --> 01:16:54.120
Yeah, so we did it there,
we did it in middle school.

01:16:54.120 --> 01:16:56.000
But then in college,

01:16:56.000 --> 01:16:58.090
performing in those Campus Crusade things,

01:16:58.090 --> 01:16:59.760
it was just like, "All right,
we're gonna get up there

01:16:59.760 --> 01:17:02.120
and we're gonna do this funny song

01:17:02.120 --> 01:17:04.690
about the unibrow or whatever together."

01:17:04.690 --> 01:17:09.690
And it was, our voices kind of went into

01:17:09.730 --> 01:17:13.520
a sort of nasally, Weird Al place that-

01:17:13.520 --> 01:17:14.380
Kind of nerdy.

01:17:14.380 --> 01:17:17.680
People talk about a lot of our early stuff

01:17:17.680 --> 01:17:19.663
sounds very Weird Al-y.

01:17:21.350 --> 01:17:22.290
And that was,

01:17:22.290 --> 01:17:24.600
it was because we had
listened to so much Weird Al.

01:17:24.600 --> 01:17:26.250
It was just, it was in our bones.

01:17:27.620 --> 01:17:31.150
Okay, so that was my
number two, that was your-

01:17:31.150 --> 01:17:32.950
So yeah, so I've taken my two,

01:17:32.950 --> 01:17:34.330
you've already taken my two,

01:17:34.330 --> 01:17:36.809
this Seinfeld Larry David
in one and Letterman.

01:17:36.809 --> 01:17:37.760
Right, so I gotta clean it up.

01:17:37.760 --> 01:17:39.750
My number three-

01:17:39.750 --> 01:17:40.850
You have three more?

01:17:40.850 --> 01:17:42.780
I have two more 'cause
Weird Al's my number two.

01:17:42.780 --> 01:17:43.613
Oh.

01:17:43.613 --> 01:17:45.941
My number three I put Questlove.

01:17:45.941 --> 01:17:47.780
Oh wow.
Like I'm a huge fan

01:17:47.780 --> 01:17:49.340
of Questlove.

01:17:49.340 --> 01:17:53.370
And just he's a huge creative inspiration

01:17:53.370 --> 01:17:58.080
that is a, within the last two years,

01:17:58.080 --> 01:18:00.170
a addition to my list.

01:18:00.170 --> 01:18:02.840
Of course, he's a hip hop icon

01:18:02.840 --> 01:18:05.976
as a member and drummer for the Roots.

01:18:05.976 --> 01:18:10.976
He's a band leader for Fallon,
which is the Roots on Fallon.

01:18:12.920 --> 01:18:16.560
But in terms of like you talk
about me wanting to be a DJ

01:18:16.560 --> 01:18:17.650
and telling that story.

01:18:17.650 --> 01:18:21.150
Like it's a product of like me connecting.

01:18:21.150 --> 01:18:22.260
You're like looking at me

01:18:22.260 --> 01:18:23.770
like you're like shocked or something.

01:18:23.770 --> 01:18:25.750
No, I'm interested to hear this

01:18:26.590 --> 01:18:28.440
'cause I would not have guessed this.

01:18:30.180 --> 01:18:35.180
Just really identifying
like how much I enjoy music

01:18:38.290 --> 01:18:40.243
on like an appreciation level.

01:18:41.930 --> 01:18:44.410
Of course with our
Merle Haggard obsession,

01:18:44.410 --> 01:18:46.200
like way back in the day,

01:18:46.200 --> 01:18:48.460
I remember I went to
Heather Wilson's house

01:18:48.460 --> 01:18:51.120
and her dad had a magazine.

01:18:51.120 --> 01:18:55.020
It was a country music heritage magazine.

01:18:55.020 --> 01:18:59.260
And it like talked about like
the best country music albums.

01:18:59.260 --> 01:19:02.630
And I was like, I remember thinking then,

01:19:02.630 --> 01:19:06.370
I took the magazine, I still have it.

01:19:06.370 --> 01:19:08.680
So like back in high
school, it was something,

01:19:08.680 --> 01:19:11.660
I was very passionate about country music

01:19:11.660 --> 01:19:15.730
as a offshoot of our
passion for Merle Haggard.

01:19:15.730 --> 01:19:17.380
But like I go to 11

01:19:17.380 --> 01:19:20.690
when it comes to like
the collection mentality

01:19:20.690 --> 01:19:24.780
and like the pre-Wiki Wiki mentality

01:19:24.780 --> 01:19:28.200
of understanding everything
about country music.

01:19:28.200 --> 01:19:30.200
And I started to think I could become

01:19:31.100 --> 01:19:33.890
like a country music museum curator.

01:19:33.890 --> 01:19:36.400
Like maybe that can be something I'll do.

01:19:36.400 --> 01:19:37.233
I remember thinking about it.

01:19:37.233 --> 01:19:39.183
That's why I kept that
magazine all these years

01:19:39.183 --> 01:19:40.337
and still have it.

01:19:40.337 --> 01:19:42.090
And then it was over the pandemic

01:19:42.090 --> 01:19:44.640
there was like a rekindling of that,

01:19:44.640 --> 01:19:47.170
which led to like the
listening party broadcast

01:19:47.170 --> 01:19:49.160
that I did with Britton
and stuff like that.

01:19:49.160 --> 01:19:53.530
But it was a result of
like me starting to watch

01:19:53.530 --> 01:19:55.640
Questlove's broadcasts on Instagram,

01:19:55.640 --> 01:19:58.070
where he would like do DJ sets

01:19:58.070 --> 01:20:01.250
and he would start talking about the music

01:20:01.250 --> 01:20:02.310
that he was playing.

01:20:02.310 --> 01:20:03.510
And it was like, I mean,

01:20:04.760 --> 01:20:08.423
he has an encyclopedic
knowledge of all these things.

01:20:12.380 --> 01:20:13.620
I'm not a fan of the Roots.

01:20:13.620 --> 01:20:15.720
I've never listened to the Roots.

01:20:15.720 --> 01:20:16.730
I'm a fan of him in the way-

01:20:16.730 --> 01:20:19.520
But they did back us up while we did our

01:20:19.520 --> 01:20:20.530
get down with Halloween rap on Fallon.

01:20:20.530 --> 01:20:21.997
Yes we did, which is-

01:20:21.997 --> 01:20:23.770
But we've been backed by the Roots

01:20:23.770 --> 01:20:26.307
on a performance before.
Which was huge.

01:20:26.307 --> 01:20:31.307
I mean, that was a career and
friendship high point for us.

01:20:31.510 --> 01:20:36.210
But like the fact that
he branches out into

01:20:37.090 --> 01:20:42.090
in middle age, he's like
five years older than us,

01:20:42.180 --> 01:20:47.103
but he's using everything
from his career to now do,

01:20:48.236 --> 01:20:50.550
to capitalize and do the things
that he's passionate about.

01:20:50.550 --> 01:20:51.560
So he's writing books,

01:20:51.560 --> 01:20:55.870
he wrote a book about creativity
called "Creative Quest,"

01:20:55.870 --> 01:20:58.010
which I've read some of.

01:20:58.010 --> 01:21:03.010
He executive produces a hip
hop documentary on an AMC

01:21:03.530 --> 01:21:08.530
called "Songs That Shook,"

01:21:08.710 --> 01:21:10.230
which is amazing.

01:21:10.230 --> 01:21:12.610
And now he's nominated for an Oscar

01:21:12.610 --> 01:21:16.630
for Best Documentary Film
for "Summer of Soul,"

01:21:16.630 --> 01:21:18.390
which is a,

01:21:18.390 --> 01:21:22.840
it's like the black Woodstock, basically,

01:21:22.840 --> 01:21:26.550
that was never, the
footage was never released.

01:21:26.550 --> 01:21:30.170
And he's nominated for an Oscar
for it and he deserves it.

01:21:30.170 --> 01:21:33.560
So the way that he's branching
out and engaging everything

01:21:33.560 --> 01:21:38.060
that up from a career
standpoint up to this point,

01:21:38.060 --> 01:21:42.900
that like he's leveraging is cool to see.

01:21:42.900 --> 01:21:46.120
And so it's very encouraging

01:21:46.120 --> 01:21:49.500
that like we talk about
the things we wanna to do,

01:21:49.500 --> 01:21:50.770
like you were doing earlier,

01:21:50.770 --> 01:21:53.610
and it's, I see a guy
like him that's like a,

01:21:53.610 --> 01:21:55.483
he's an elder statesman of music.

01:21:55.483 --> 01:22:00.483
And he doesn't just do one thing.

01:22:00.910 --> 01:22:02.150
He doesn't just do one thing.

01:22:02.150 --> 01:22:03.200
He does a lot.

01:22:03.200 --> 01:22:05.330
So when we think about our legacy

01:22:05.330 --> 01:22:08.290
and we think about the things
that we have yet to do,

01:22:08.290 --> 01:22:11.950
those are two different
things but they interplay.

01:22:11.950 --> 01:22:14.520
So even if it's something
like us investing

01:22:14.520 --> 01:22:16.390
in the next generation of creators

01:22:16.390 --> 01:22:18.923
with our Mythical Creator Accelerator,

01:22:20.020 --> 01:22:21.797
it's just another example of,

01:22:21.797 --> 01:22:25.517
"Hey, we're gonna pay it forward."

01:22:26.950 --> 01:22:30.860
But we're still here for
it. We're not retiring.

01:22:30.860 --> 01:22:31.693
We got a lot to do.

01:22:32.690 --> 01:22:33.883
So yeah.

01:22:34.797 --> 01:22:36.070
Questlove.
And I met him.

01:22:36.070 --> 01:22:36.903
We met him once.

01:22:36.903 --> 01:22:40.410
If I'd have known that you
felt that way about Questlove

01:22:41.390 --> 01:22:43.860
when he was playing the
drums for us rapping,

01:22:43.860 --> 01:22:46.010
then I would've probably
been more nervous.

01:22:47.417 --> 01:22:49.800
I came to grips with it
afterward, I wasn't as-

01:22:49.800 --> 01:22:50.633
Okay, it was after.

01:22:50.633 --> 01:22:53.340
Yeah, 'cause it was more pandemic related.

01:22:53.340 --> 01:22:55.730
All right, so I just have my number one,

01:22:55.730 --> 01:22:57.250
which is for both of us.

01:22:57.250 --> 01:22:58.993
You don't have any left.

01:22:59.890 --> 01:23:01.770
Yeah, but I mean, I gotta...

01:23:03.120 --> 01:23:04.750
You want me to guess who it is?

01:23:04.750 --> 01:23:06.213
Yeah.

01:23:06.213 --> 01:23:07.850
Is it Wes Anderson?

01:23:07.850 --> 01:23:10.350
No, I went in a totally
different direction.

01:23:10.350 --> 01:23:12.049
'Cause I was like, he was
on my honorable mention,

01:23:12.049 --> 01:23:13.790
but I was like, Link loves
Wes Anderson so much,

01:23:13.790 --> 01:23:14.908
is gonna put him number one?

01:23:14.908 --> 01:23:16.058
But I was like, but as your list goes on,

01:23:16.058 --> 01:23:17.548
I'm like that can't be.

01:23:17.548 --> 01:23:19.810
Okay, and this is for both of us.

01:23:19.810 --> 01:23:21.140
You're not gonna disagree with this,

01:23:21.140 --> 01:23:23.600
because again, with
everything we've talked about.

01:23:23.600 --> 01:23:26.180
My number one, I'm putting Benny Enzor.

01:23:26.180 --> 01:23:27.013
Whoa!

01:23:28.110 --> 01:23:29.610
Yes, baby!

01:23:29.610 --> 01:23:32.300
Wow. Yeah, okay.

01:23:32.300 --> 01:23:34.750
I mean, yeah. I mean it
just didn't cross my mind.

01:23:34.750 --> 01:23:36.730
But now that I I've said it-
But in reality, yes.

01:23:36.730 --> 01:23:38.580
It totally makes sense, right?

01:23:38.580 --> 01:23:39.413
Oh yeah.

01:23:39.413 --> 01:23:41.530
Okay, maybe you've heard his name,

01:23:41.530 --> 01:23:43.330
maybe you know from the
"Book of Mythicality"

01:23:43.330 --> 01:23:44.530
I was talking about him

01:23:44.530 --> 01:23:47.670
or all the times we talked
about our high school band,

01:23:47.670 --> 01:23:50.650
the Wax Paper Dogz ending in a Z.

01:23:50.650 --> 01:23:51.673
It was the '90s.

