he played the piano at the
church that we went to.

01:24:07.960 --> 01:24:10.000
We always knew him growing up.

01:24:10.000 --> 01:24:12.090
He was one of the youth leaders

01:24:12.090 --> 01:24:13.920
and also a dad.
A soccer coach.

01:24:13.920 --> 01:24:18.480
He was a soccer coach
when my mom got divorced

01:24:18.480 --> 01:24:22.650
and me and her moved into our
own places, the two of us,

01:24:22.650 --> 01:24:25.350
it was across the street from the Enzors.

01:24:25.350 --> 01:24:27.000
Which they still live over there.

01:24:28.110 --> 01:24:29.810
So it's like in the small community,

01:24:29.810 --> 01:24:34.810
he was very present at church,
at soccer, in the community.

01:24:35.610 --> 01:24:38.320
And then at school later on.

01:24:38.320 --> 01:24:40.230
That's right, and then he worked,

01:24:40.230 --> 01:24:43.010
he was like the guy who in
charge of the detention.

01:24:43.010 --> 01:24:44.567
Which I had to go to.

01:24:44.567 --> 01:24:45.400
At the high school.

01:24:45.400 --> 01:24:48.140
I had to go to detention
and the guy in charge of it

01:24:48.140 --> 01:24:50.833
was my keyboard player.

01:24:50.833 --> 01:24:53.043
Yeah, so it's like Benny Enzor started,

01:24:53.880 --> 01:24:58.880
through the church he
started a cafe for the,

01:24:59.060 --> 01:25:00.560
like a coffee shop for-

01:25:00.560 --> 01:25:03.430
Yeah, this is your, this is true.

01:25:03.430 --> 01:25:05.980
I'm just, I didn't think about it,

01:25:05.980 --> 01:25:07.820
but now that you're putting
it all together, yes.

01:25:07.820 --> 01:25:09.200
'Cause I know we didn't, besides your dad,

01:25:09.200 --> 01:25:10.033
we didn't think of it

01:25:10.033 --> 01:25:12.790
in terms of personal
interpersonal relationships.

01:25:12.790 --> 01:25:14.560
We talked about it in terms of like

01:25:14.560 --> 01:25:18.120
external celebrity influences.

01:25:18.120 --> 01:25:21.220
But yeah, he started the
Maranatha Cafe coffee shop

01:25:21.220 --> 01:25:24.130
to have Campbell University
students could hang out,

01:25:24.130 --> 01:25:27.810
and it was a way for the church
to have to create a space,

01:25:27.810 --> 01:25:30.690
a safe space for them to
hang out, listen to music.

01:25:30.690 --> 01:25:34.930
There was an open mic night, which then we

01:25:34.930 --> 01:25:36.990
as high school students
involved in the church,

01:25:36.990 --> 01:25:40.820
we helped facilitate, move chairs around.

01:25:40.820 --> 01:25:44.860
We helped renovate the
building and decorate it.

01:25:44.860 --> 01:25:49.860
And Benny was a huge
music fan and musician.

01:25:51.070 --> 01:25:54.460
He's a great pianist, also played guitar.

01:25:54.460 --> 01:25:58.230
And huge Allman Brothers fan from like...

01:25:58.230 --> 01:26:00.230
And so he was a hippie.

01:26:00.230 --> 01:26:01.930
He kinda, well, he had a hippie vibe,

01:26:01.930 --> 01:26:03.710
he had longer shaggy hair.

01:26:03.710 --> 01:26:07.153
He didn't look like any
of our other parents.

01:26:09.017 --> 01:26:12.930
And he would, he took
all of his old records

01:26:12.930 --> 01:26:15.300
and then we decorated the Maranatha Cafe

01:26:15.300 --> 01:26:17.110
with all of his records.

01:26:17.110 --> 01:26:18.780
So it wasn't Christian music.

01:26:18.780 --> 01:26:23.440
It was like '70s albums wall to wall,

01:26:23.440 --> 01:26:24.650
that became the wallpaper.

01:26:24.650 --> 01:26:28.433
It was like super cool
and it was his baby.

01:26:29.790 --> 01:26:32.450
Of course it was because of the-

01:26:32.450 --> 01:26:33.790
Open mic night.

01:26:33.790 --> 01:26:36.170
The fact that the Maranatha Cafe existed

01:26:36.170 --> 01:26:38.740
and there was this, yeah, open mic night.

01:26:38.740 --> 01:26:42.590
Open mic night, that was
what gave us the courage

01:26:42.590 --> 01:26:44.340
or the opportunity, we
didn't need the courage,

01:26:44.340 --> 01:26:45.367
the opportunity to be like,

01:26:45.367 --> 01:26:47.040
"Well, of course we're going to perform

01:26:47.040 --> 01:26:48.490
at the open mic night."

01:26:48.490 --> 01:26:52.890
And that was when Eric
Woodruff had a guitar

01:26:52.890 --> 01:26:55.000
and he could play the guitar.

01:26:55.000 --> 01:26:56.570
John Enzor had a bass,

01:26:56.570 --> 01:26:57.700
he was learning bass guitar.
But he was learning.

01:26:57.700 --> 01:26:58.533
He was definitely learning.

01:26:58.533 --> 01:26:59.510
And of course Eric was learning too,

01:26:59.510 --> 01:27:01.833
but he was the most
proficient at the time.

01:27:02.721 --> 01:27:07.000
And I remember the
first song we ever wrote

01:27:07.000 --> 01:27:10.450
was about the, ironically,

01:27:10.450 --> 01:27:14.010
given specifically my
deconstruction story,

01:27:14.010 --> 01:27:16.000
was about creation.

01:27:16.000 --> 01:27:17.580
And it was basically about how creation

01:27:17.580 --> 01:27:20.100
proves that God exists.

01:27:20.100 --> 01:27:21.570
And that it happened in seven days.

01:27:21.570 --> 01:27:22.700
Yeah, yeah, and it-

01:27:22.700 --> 01:27:25.850
But the reason why-
We made fun of organic soup

01:27:25.850 --> 01:27:29.250
and spontaneous life, but it was-

01:27:29.250 --> 01:27:31.110
We had to write Christian.

01:27:31.110 --> 01:27:32.400
It was horrible.
Oh yeah.

01:27:32.400 --> 01:27:33.670
We had to write Christian songs

01:27:33.670 --> 01:27:34.667
because to play the open mic night,

01:27:34.667 --> 01:27:36.610
you did have to play Christian music.

01:27:36.610 --> 01:27:37.570
It was a Christian cafe.

01:27:37.570 --> 01:27:38.850
But that's where Benny comes in

01:27:38.850 --> 01:27:43.483
because Benny listened to our
creation song and was like,

01:27:45.107 --> 01:27:47.090
"Boys, you know if you're really serious

01:27:47.090 --> 01:27:49.140
about playing music,

01:27:49.140 --> 01:27:51.750
you should probably learn
how to play cover songs.

01:27:51.750 --> 01:27:53.650
Like you should learn
how to play other songs

01:27:53.650 --> 01:27:55.120
that have been written."

01:27:55.120 --> 01:27:55.953
And he was also like,

01:27:55.953 --> 01:27:58.410
"And I've got some old songs
that I wrote like 20 years ago

01:27:58.410 --> 01:28:00.090
that you could also play."

01:28:00.090 --> 01:28:01.880
And so that was the beginning of us.

01:28:01.880 --> 01:28:04.100
'Cause again, most people who sit down,

01:28:04.100 --> 01:28:06.190
even if you listen to music a lot,

01:28:06.190 --> 01:28:07.250
you sit down to write a song,

01:28:07.250 --> 01:28:09.330
even if you can play a few chords,

01:28:09.330 --> 01:28:10.780
you don't understand song structure.

01:28:10.780 --> 01:28:11.613
You don't understand like,

01:28:11.613 --> 01:28:14.340
"Oh, there's this number
of measures in the verse

01:28:14.340 --> 01:28:16.000
and then there's a bridge
and there's a chorus."

01:28:16.000 --> 01:28:18.640
Like there's a structure that it follows.

01:28:18.640 --> 01:28:20.570
If you just start
writing, you have no idea.

01:28:20.570 --> 01:28:24.067
Benny saw that immediately
and then was like,

01:28:24.067 --> 01:28:24.950
"Here's some songs.

01:28:24.950 --> 01:28:26.070
Like, hey, can you just look?

01:28:26.070 --> 01:28:27.150
What if you just played

01:28:27.150 --> 01:28:30.560
'I Can See Clearly Now the Rain is Gone.'

01:28:30.560 --> 01:28:32.120
Or "Country Roads.'"

01:28:32.120 --> 01:28:33.390
By John Denver.

01:28:33.390 --> 01:28:35.900
And it was, that's how
he learned how to sing.

01:28:35.900 --> 01:28:37.520
And that's how I learned
how to play the guitar

01:28:37.520 --> 01:28:38.792
because it's-

01:28:38.792 --> 01:28:42.290
But he also said, "And I
can also be in the band

01:28:42.290 --> 01:28:44.800
to help you actually
make this into a thing."

01:28:44.800 --> 01:28:46.273
To like keep the beat.

01:28:47.387 --> 01:28:50.470
"I'll play keyboard, and
like if you need a beat,

01:28:50.470 --> 01:28:51.870
there'll be a beat on my keyboard."

01:28:51.870 --> 01:28:53.490
We didn't have a drummer yet.

01:28:53.490 --> 01:28:55.301
Early in the day, right?

01:28:55.301 --> 01:28:57.380
And we started writing songs.

01:28:57.380 --> 01:28:59.930
He brought out, yeah, like
he had all these songs

01:28:59.930 --> 01:29:03.320
that he had written in the
'70s, like handwritten,

01:29:03.320 --> 01:29:05.610
and he like taught him to us.

01:29:05.610 --> 01:29:07.460
And we sang them.

01:29:07.460 --> 01:29:11.990
And it was like Allman
Brothers meets John Denver

01:29:11.990 --> 01:29:12.930
sung by two guys

01:29:12.930 --> 01:29:16.040
who were trying to sound
like Hank Williams Jr.

01:29:16.040 --> 01:29:17.580
Right, it was a train wreck.

01:29:17.580 --> 01:29:19.450
I think there's a whole other podcast

01:29:19.450 --> 01:29:23.580
just about Wax Paper Dogz,
but I just wanna say that like

01:29:23.580 --> 01:29:26.650
we threw ourselves into this band

01:29:26.650 --> 01:29:29.400
and because there was an adult in the room

01:29:29.400 --> 01:29:33.563
who we considered, it
was like he was a peer.

01:29:34.420 --> 01:29:37.750
He was John, Matt did our sound
and did all the tech stuff.

01:29:37.750 --> 01:29:40.730
But we were a group of peers

01:29:40.730 --> 01:29:43.430
and he didn't elevate himself above us.

01:29:43.430 --> 01:29:46.040
And we had so much fun. We
threw ourselves into it.

01:29:46.040 --> 01:29:48.360
We committed so wholeheartedly to it.

01:29:48.360 --> 01:29:50.730
And there's something
about being in a band

01:29:50.730 --> 01:29:53.170
that really helps you develop creatively.

01:29:53.170 --> 01:29:58.170
We were making thousands
of creative decisions

01:29:58.170 --> 01:30:00.400
in every time we would get together.

01:30:00.400 --> 01:30:04.530
And so you start to learn to
work with four other people.

01:30:04.530 --> 01:30:07.790
And you learn how, you learn
to trust yourself creatively.

01:30:07.790 --> 01:30:09.170
And you learn to put yourself out there.

01:30:09.170 --> 01:30:13.780
'Cause what ended up happening
is within that first year,

01:30:13.780 --> 01:30:18.780
we went from an older
guy giving us his music

01:30:19.530 --> 01:30:22.840
and having the drums be on the keyboard

01:30:22.840 --> 01:30:27.840
to me and you sitting in a
room somewhere, writing a song.

01:30:27.940 --> 01:30:29.850
And we started writing.

01:30:29.850 --> 01:30:31.530
We would've never had
the courage to do that

01:30:31.530 --> 01:30:32.363
without his influence.
Yeah,

01:30:32.363 --> 01:30:33.390
you and I were writing music

01:30:33.390 --> 01:30:37.530
for the Wax Paper Dogz together,
writing music and lyrics.

01:30:37.530 --> 01:30:38.950
And then the other thing we were doing

01:30:38.950 --> 01:30:41.900
is we were making them a little funny.

01:30:41.900 --> 01:30:45.280
Like they all had to be like veiled.

01:30:45.280 --> 01:30:48.160
Well, some of them were
like overt Christian,

01:30:48.160 --> 01:30:50.000
and some of them were veiled.

01:30:50.000 --> 01:30:52.380
And some of them started to get funny.

01:30:52.380 --> 01:30:53.443
And that's where we-

01:30:54.510 --> 01:30:57.757
The only surviving
recording actually is funny,

01:30:57.757 --> 01:30:58.590
it's a little bit funny.

01:30:58.590 --> 01:31:00.980
It's a racial reconciliation song.

01:31:00.980 --> 01:31:03.440
But it has a reference
to professional wrestler.

01:31:03.440 --> 01:31:06.860
Right, so I mean, we can get into all this

01:31:06.860 --> 01:31:08.000
in more detail later,

01:31:08.000 --> 01:31:11.360
but for now, just like we did
in the "Book of Mythicality,"

01:31:11.360 --> 01:31:13.930
we're like giving
throwback thanks to Benny

01:31:13.930 --> 01:31:16.130
because in a lot of ways,

01:31:16.130 --> 01:31:17.920
I don't think we would be here.

01:31:17.920 --> 01:31:21.870
Because as creators, he
really fanned that flame

01:31:21.870 --> 01:31:26.020
and gave us a sense of
identity as creative people

01:31:26.020 --> 01:31:29.053
that translated into what we wore,

01:31:30.050 --> 01:31:31.500
the music we listened to.

01:31:31.500 --> 01:31:34.690
By the time we went off
to college, we looked,

01:31:34.690 --> 01:31:37.400
we have fully embraced like
the '90s alternative thing,

01:31:37.400 --> 01:31:39.390
unlike anybody in our high school

01:31:39.390 --> 01:31:42.040
and unlike anybody we
associated with in college.

01:31:42.040 --> 01:31:45.720
So it wasn't just an artistic thing.

01:31:45.720 --> 01:31:49.460
It was a complete identity.

01:31:49.460 --> 01:31:51.400
It set up our personalities in some ways

01:31:51.400 --> 01:31:56.400
of always feeling like
we didn't quite fit,

01:31:56.690 --> 01:31:59.830
but kind of taking a little
bit of pride in that, I think,

01:31:59.830 --> 01:32:01.620
to be transparent about it.

01:32:01.620 --> 01:32:02.550
Oh yeah.

01:32:02.550 --> 01:32:03.760
Yeah, that's a good one.

01:32:03.760 --> 01:32:08.060
I think Benny will be, I texted
with John not too long ago,

01:32:08.060 --> 01:32:08.893
so I'll let him know

01:32:08.893 --> 01:32:11.960
that Benny gets the shout out in this one.

01:32:11.960 --> 01:32:12.933
Love you, Benny.

01:32:13.940 --> 01:32:14.773
Now, okay.

01:32:17.270 --> 01:32:18.790
We talked, we went ahead

01:32:18.790 --> 01:32:20.510
and talked ahead of time about one thing.

01:32:20.510 --> 01:32:23.370
We didn't give any heads up on
who was gonna be on our list.

01:32:23.370 --> 01:32:26.310
But the thing that we
had a conversation about

01:32:26.310 --> 01:32:31.290
was that if this list was in fact factual,

01:32:31.290 --> 01:32:35.310
that we would be each
other's number one because-

01:32:35.310 --> 01:32:36.930
So we can be each other's zero.

01:32:36.930 --> 01:32:38.030
So we'll be each other's zero.

01:32:38.030 --> 01:32:42.890
We obviously the most
significant creative influence

01:32:42.890 --> 01:32:44.980
that is embodied in a person

01:32:44.980 --> 01:32:46.910
in each of our lives is each other

01:32:46.910 --> 01:32:51.910
because we don't do any
creativity for the most part

01:32:52.060 --> 01:32:54.360
where we're not directly
consulting with one another

01:32:54.360 --> 01:32:57.880
and it's a product of our
collective imagination.

01:32:57.880 --> 01:33:01.926
Yeah, I mean, and I'll also
give a shout out to Stevie.

01:33:01.926 --> 01:33:04.650
In terms of it's like

01:33:05.590 --> 01:33:07.580
she's become a creative soundboard for us

01:33:07.580 --> 01:33:09.750
that like we hatch things.

01:33:09.750 --> 01:33:14.080
And then we always know that
there's like the next phase

01:33:14.080 --> 01:33:15.880
is getting Stevie's buy-in

01:33:15.880 --> 01:33:18.610
and like her applying her filters

01:33:18.610 --> 01:33:22.330
and first reaction to the
next thing we're hatching.

01:33:22.330 --> 01:33:23.290
Yeah, Stevie has been-

01:33:23.290 --> 01:33:26.200
And then obviously shaping creatively

01:33:26.200 --> 01:33:27.940
everything you see at Mythical

01:33:29.220 --> 01:33:31.920
that we're involved in or not.

01:33:31.920 --> 01:33:34.470
She's the first filter besides each other

01:33:34.470 --> 01:33:35.520
for everything we do.

01:33:36.800 --> 01:33:39.830
I wanna shout out
Jessie, my wife, as well.

01:33:39.830 --> 01:33:40.877
I tend to discuss that-

01:33:40.877 --> 01:33:43.490
I love that we're using
the term shout out.

01:33:43.490 --> 01:33:45.280
We're bringing it back.

01:33:45.280 --> 01:33:47.230
I wanna give heartbeat props to my wife

01:33:49.500 --> 01:33:54.070
because I very rarely think
about anything creatively

01:33:54.070 --> 01:33:56.950
that I don't consult her about.

01:33:56.950 --> 01:33:59.250
Anything that I write, I
give to her when I'm done.

01:33:59.250 --> 01:34:02.370
And even if I get stuck
creatively a lot of times

01:34:02.370 --> 01:34:04.670
'cause I do a lot of stuff at home too.

01:34:04.670 --> 01:34:05.940
I do a lot of writing at home

01:34:05.940 --> 01:34:08.390
'cause I don't have enough
time to do it at work.

01:34:09.480 --> 01:34:11.470
So she's been a huge influence.

01:34:11.470 --> 01:34:14.230
And also, go back to the fact

01:34:14.230 --> 01:34:15.900
that both of our wives were the ones

01:34:15.900 --> 01:34:20.370
who sort of encouraged us
to make a creative thing

01:34:20.370 --> 01:34:22.490
our profession back in the day, so.

01:34:22.490 --> 01:34:23.563
Oh, absolutely.

01:34:24.440 --> 01:34:25.530
But yeah, and I'm saying,

01:34:25.530 --> 01:34:29.180
so we'll make each other
our zero on the list,

01:34:29.180 --> 01:34:31.050
which sounds bad but I think it's good

01:34:31.050 --> 01:34:36.050
'cause there's like, there's
not any, at every single point.

01:34:36.350 --> 01:34:38.010
I surprised you with the Questlove thing,

01:34:38.010 --> 01:34:42.680
but basically there's so much overlap.

01:34:42.680 --> 01:34:47.680
It's like 99.9% creative
overlap over so many years

01:34:48.450 --> 01:34:52.050
that like we touch so
much that even the things

01:34:52.050 --> 01:34:53.260
that we don't touch

01:34:54.120 --> 01:34:56.170
in the other person's creative endeavors,

01:34:57.590 --> 01:34:58.430
the presence will be felt.

01:34:58.430 --> 01:35:00.860
Well, which is why you'll
probably relate to,

01:35:00.860 --> 01:35:02.930
I'm just gonna quickly run
through the honorable mentions,

01:35:02.930 --> 01:35:05.190
the ones that were in the initial batch,

01:35:05.190 --> 01:35:06.130
but then got pushed off.

01:35:06.130 --> 01:35:10.523
Wes Anderson, Spike
Jones, Quentin Tarantino,

01:35:11.540 --> 01:35:16.540
Rob Reiner, Donald Glover,
Errol Morris, as I said,

01:35:16.610 --> 01:35:20.870
The Lonely Island, Taika Waititi,

01:35:20.870 --> 01:35:23.703
Robert Rodriguez, the Duplass brothers,

01:35:24.810 --> 01:35:28.200
Will Smith, and Werner Herzog.

01:35:28.200 --> 01:35:30.470
Okay, all right. Well
let's wrap this thing up.

01:35:30.470 --> 01:35:32.570
This has been a long one.

01:35:32.570 --> 01:35:35.500
So I guess I stole your
rec, "Sherman's March."

01:35:35.500 --> 01:35:37.580
But so let's just make this a double rec,

01:35:37.580 --> 01:35:39.750
'cause we mentioned two
things that are really good.

01:35:39.750 --> 01:35:41.728
The first thing is Ross McElwee's,

01:35:41.728 --> 01:35:45.847
which it looks like
McElwee, M-C-E-L-W-E-E.

01:35:45.847 --> 01:35:48.380
"Sherman's March." Great documentary.

01:35:48.380 --> 01:35:50.663
I'm sure you can get it
on Amazon or whatever.

01:35:51.720 --> 01:35:53.997
And then that Letterman book,

01:35:53.997 --> 01:35:55.600
"The Last Giant of Late Night."

01:35:55.600 --> 01:35:57.310
I think if you're in a creative profession

01:35:57.310 --> 01:35:59.170
and you're just trying to figure out

01:35:59.170 --> 01:36:02.080
how to like define yourself creatively,

01:36:02.080 --> 01:36:03.440
or you've got a creative endeavor

01:36:03.440 --> 01:36:05.320
that you're currently in the middle of,

01:36:05.320 --> 01:36:08.207
I think that can be a
pretty inspirational book.

01:36:08.207 --> 01:36:10.230
"David Letterman: The
Last Giant of Late Night."

01:36:10.230 --> 01:36:11.420
All right. It's been a fun one.

01:36:11.420 --> 01:36:15.780
Hopefully you're inspired, your
creative juices are flowing.

01:36:15.780 --> 01:36:18.630
Tell us who your creative
influences are, #EarBiscuits.

